,1.4011662473107581962690469011156599875976542,5.1512092243024828013158947236980013451768783,0,0,0,5.1512092243024828013158947236980013451768783\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Early operating cash before payments,5.1512092243024828013158947236980013451768783,1.4167862981614740355628496890018102800358132,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915,0,0,0,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Working-capital investment or release,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915,0E-42,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915,0,0,0,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Deposit release only against eligible expansion,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915,0.000000,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915,0,0,0,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Maintenance and scheduled growth capex,6.5679955224639568368787444126998116252126915,-0.78241230977205111448163382444259751355177145,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200,0,0,0,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Upfront scheduled capex GST with no cash refund assumed,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200,0.0000,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200,0,0,0,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Interest and term principal,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200,0E-46,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200,0,0,0,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Remaining operating cash after payments,5.7855832126919057223971105882572141116609200,1.4167862981614740355628496890018102800358132,7.2023695108533797579599602772590243916967332,0,0,0,7.2023695108533797579599602772590243916967332\\r\\np_1360586c72c18cf3e29d,base,True,5,2031-10-20,Segregate continuation liquidity reserve before terminal valuation,7.2023695108533797579599602772590243916967332,-1.3537978114168876180549898553383957151825539,5.8485716994364921399049704219206286765141793,0,0,0,5.8485716994364921399049704219206286765141793\\r\\n\"","\"# Rachit Prints Limited\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n**Hold — unresolved. A capacity-expansion and cash-conversion thesis, not an evidence-backed compounder.** Rachit makes specialty mattress fabrics and has shifted toward higher-value knitted fabric. Operations grew at 17.36% over FY21–FY26, but FY26's ₹53.24 crore sales produced only ₹4.58 crore PAT and negative ₹3.60 crore operating cash flow. The decisive concern is not the small listing: it is the combination of customer-group concentration, substantial related-party advances/receivables, unspent IPO expansion money, thin available cash, and recurring reporting-control weaknesses. At the indicative 19 October 2026 price of ₹170, reported P/E is 18.32×, which does not establish a margin of safety. BQS, GQS and IAS remain **NS**, with disclosed coverage and bounds. Current bank liquidity, independently reconciled cash vintages, recoverability of connected-party balances and verified completion/economics of the still-unspent IPO object are prerequisites for a favorable investment conclusion. A cheaper quote alone cannot resolve these gaps.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n- **Exact security:** Rachit Prints Limited; ISIN **INE0ZN101029**; BSE SME code **544503**, symbol **RACHIT**. This is not Rachit Finance or another similarly named issuer. Incorporated 31 March 2003; predecessor business dates to 1985; public-company conversion in July 2024. Listed 8 September 2025. [S1–S4]\\n- **Information cutoff:** 20 October 2026. **Market date:** 19 October 2026. Research assembled 23 October from documents already published before cutoff. Later price/shareholding observations are not used to revise the snapshot.\\n- **Indicative price:** ₹170, corroborated by two secondary dated observations. **Current shares:** 4,937,100. **Market capitalization:** ₹83.93 crore. Lot: 1,000 shares, corroborated by the official interop reference; this is **not evidence of an NSE listing or a verified BSE trading tape**. [S1,S5,S6]\\n- Standalone Indian GAAP / Accounting Standards, not Ind AS. No subsidiary, associate or joint venture identified in the reviewed issuer records. Manufacturing/textiles rubric. All financial tables are ₹ crore unless stated; financial statements denominated in hundreds were divided by 100,000.\\n- Five-year investor horizon to 20 October 2031; nominal INR equity hurdle 15%, tested at 12% and 18%. No interim distributions assumed. ₹5 lakh liquidity reference is methodological, not a recommended position for this user.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Recurring control/disclosure weakness and connected-party cash exposure. No substantiated diversion or serious minority abuse established. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Historical cash vintages, FY23 stock records, recurring audit-trail limitation and financial-note inconsistencies require clarification. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Actual March-to-entry cash and bank availability are unknown. A simulated floor breach is not an actual default; optional rescue is not committed. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | No primary BSE20/60-session series or current restrictions. The NSE reference is not a BSE tape. |\\n\\n**Confidence:** High on issuer, actual IPO share count and the transcription of FY25/FY26 signed statements; Medium on underlying revenue history, customer concentration and reported profits; Low on clean incremental returns, actual entry-date liquidity, current BSE execution conditions, and supported intrinsic return. Unmodified opinions do not erase specific audit-trail observations. Missing original FY23/FY24 full audit reports are not substituted with a management summary or an IPO restatement opinion.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\nFramework: **Small / Micro-Cap Stock Research Master Prompt v3.0**, verified SHA256 `68d4de4255427df276318d0231530b867a453e740c74150e9768bf1387224137`. Nine business/growth factors plus R/D/P/L are scored once and reused. Decimal arithmetic uses 44-digit precision; display rounding is half-up, never classification input. No missing factor is replaced with a midpoint or redistributed weight.\\n\\n| Score | Known sum | Coverage | Raw / final | Uncapped bound | Capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.6400 | 80.00% | NS / NS | 3.8400 to 5.6400 | 3.8400 to 5.6400 |\\n| GQS | 4.8500 | 85.00% | NS / NS | 5.0000 to 6.3500 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 |\\n| IAS | 0.6000 | 20.00% | NS / NS | 1.4000 to 8.6000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n\\nBQS weights are GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS weights are REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS weights are final BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%.\\n\\nFor each score, known contribution K and missing weight m produce the missing-evidence interval **[K+m, K+10m]**, not a confidence interval. BQS has K=3.64, m=0.20, so 3.84–5.64. GQS has K=4.85, m=0.15, so uncapped 5.00–6.35. **Persistent CASH3 caps GQS at 5; the collapsed 5.00–5.00 bound does not turn an incomplete score into a scored 5.0.** This is supported by weak multi-year cash realization under both cash vintages, not merely FY26 expansion spending. IAS has only D scored, so generic bounds are 1.40–8.60. Propagating the known BQS bounds narrows the dependency-aware IAS range to **2.11–7.51**, still NS. No active governance/funding failure cap is asserted: G1 is Conditional and actual funding failure is unproven.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | BQS term | GQS term | IAS term |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | — | — |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | — | — |\\n| REV | 7 | Medium | 0.8400 | 2.8000 | — |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | 0.5000 | 1.0000 | — |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | NS | NS | — |\\n| CASH | 3 | Medium | 0.3000 | 0.3000 | — |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | NS | — | — |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | 0.4000 | — | — |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | — | 0.7500 | — |\\n| R | NS | Low | — | — | NS |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | — | — | 0.6000 |\\n| P | NS | Low | — | — | NS |\\n| L | NS | Low | — | — | NS |\\n\\n**GOV:** Recurring disclosure and control weakness, and concentrated connected-party cash, without demonstrated diversion. S1: The audit-trail exception recurs despite unmodified statutory and IFC opinions. S1: Elite receivables and advances total ₹4.7972 crore; purchases from CFO-owned Mirahi total ₹1.7544 crore. Commercial terms have not been independently verified. S1/S2: AOC2, EPS and tax notes contain inconsistencies. S7 records historical information non-cooperation. S16 confirms shareholder approval of the notice remuneration revisions. The voting denominator presentation is inconsistent and is not used to alter issued shares. **Counterevidence:** Auditors did not report fraud or default. The RPTs are disclosed, and no material diversion has been substantiated. These facts do not justify an automatic GOV3 or G1 failure.\\n\\n**MOAT:** Customization and repeat customers are useful but contestable, with negotiating power concentrated downstream. S3: Seven of the top ten customers had relationships exceeding ten years. S3/S8/S9: The Sheela Foam/Kurlon group represented 50.19% of FY25 revenue, while the top five customers represented 82.55%. S3: Non-exclusive MoUs contain no guaranteed orders. Standard imported machinery does not establish a proprietary cost or technology advantage. **Counterevidence:** Knitted-product growth and the FY25 sales-return rate of 0.28% demonstrate customer acceptance. The business is not wholly undifferentiated.\\n\\n**REV:** The preferred five-year comparable revenue CAGR, FY21–FY26, falls in the 15–20% band. No trajectory adjustment. S4/S3/S2/S1: Operating revenue rose from ₹23.9094 crore to ₹53.2399 crore at an unchanged standalone perimeter, with no acquisition identified. The FY23–FY26 three-year cross-check falls in the same 15–20% band. Manufacturing dominates the FY26 addition. S15 reports approximate nine-month quantity growth of 19.42% and value growth of 28.13%; product-mix and full-year price/volume attribution remain unavailable. TAM earns no score bonus. **Counterevidence:** The pandemic-affected FY21 base and FY23 stock-record limitation reduce confidence. Connected-party sales contribute to the latest increment.\\n\\n**DUR:** There is real access to mattress-fabric demand, limited by customer concentration and unverified economics of further capacity. S3: Knitted-fabric capacity grew and reported FY25 utilization was 100%. S14: Six machines were commissioned in December 2025, increasing stated daily knitted-fabric capacity from 6,300 to 9,300 metres. S1/S3: The separate 12-machine IPO object was unspent at March 2026; its quoted price expired in October 2025 and completion has not been verified. **Counterevidence:** The completed six-machine cohort, FY26 PPE investment and manufactured-sales growth demonstrate expansion activity. No blanket assertion that every growth program stalled is justified.\\n\\n**CAP:** NS: Additional-capital returns cannot be separated reliably into installed cohorts, working-capital reclassifications and trough recovery. Three- and five-year NOPAT/capital bridges are provided as diagnostics, not clean unit returns. FY26 book capital grew sharply while operating EBIT improved only modestly. FY23 stock records, FY24 cash/debt vintages and non-operating balances impair precise capital attribution. **Counterevidence:** Older assets and customers generated a strong FY24–FY25 profit recovery. This does not prove that all new investment destroys value.\\n\\n**CASH:** Persistent weak realization, not just one expansion year: five-year conversion is weak under both original and favorable restated CFO presentations. FY23 CFO was negative; FY25 CFO was near zero despite ₹4.56 crore PAT; FY26 CFO was negative ₹3.60 crore. FY24 positive CFO included stock/advance releases and classification changes, rather than a demonstrated steady cash engine. S1: Aged and disputed receivables remain classified as good, alongside substantial supplier and related-party advances. **Counterevidence:** FY26 investment could produce later collections, and FY24 did include genuine working-capital release. Nevertheless capex does not explain negative CFO, and the multi-year pattern supports the GQS cap at5.\\n\\n**BAL:** NS: Actual entry cash and confirmed bank availability are missing. The March balance sheet alone cannot establish survival. S1: Available cash before further haircut is ₹0.2275 crore, against annual term principal of ₹2.5246 crore. S1: The ₹4.4 crore IPO deposit is earmarked; the ₹1.9657 crore SBI deposit is pledged; drawn debt is ₹10.8979 crore. No automatic access to an undrawn facility, released collateral, subsidy or promoter rescue is assumed. **Counterevidence:** Post-IPO equity of ₹34.8962 crore, moderate accounting leverage and no reported default argue against claiming proven insolvency.\\n\\n**EXEC:** Mixed delivery: demonstrated product scaling and a completed six-machine cohort, against the delayed IPO object and weak cash delivery. S3/S14: Knitted output grew from 1.221 to 1.885 million metres in FY23–FY25; six further machines were commissioned on 1 December 2025. S1/S3: The RHP indicated FY26 procurement for the 12-machine IPO object, versus zero capex-object utilization at March 2026. The completed six cannot be mapped to that object without invoices and funding records. FY26 manufacturing growth did not deliver current-share earnings or cash progress. S15 corroborates nine-month sales quantity growth, but does not establish realized output or utilization by machine cohort. **Counterevidence:** The completed six machines increased stated daily capacity by 3,000 metres. Together with ₹9.10 crore FY26 cash capex, this supports EXEC5 rather than the earlier incomplete-evidence score of 4. Realized line-level utilization and cohort returns remain unknown.\\n\\n**EARN:** Profits are mainly operating, but latest per-share progress is mixed and normalization matters. FY26 other income of ₹0.278 crore is small relative to ₹6.210 crore PBT; gains are not the dominant source. FY26 revenue rose27.7% but PAT only0.44%, as depreciation and financing costs increased. The IPO increased the current share denominator36.08%. Current-share EPS is ₹9.28 versus FY25 closing-share EPS of ₹12.57. **Counterevidence:** The FY23–FY25 operating-profit recovery and current interest coverage support real earnings. Weak cash alone is not evidence of fabricated profit.\\n\\n**R:** NS: A supported base-case investor return cannot be established without actual entry liquidity and financing evidence. The five-year cash models are explicitly conditional. Every unfunded breach suppresses ordinary IRR; optional recapitalization is never called committed. The indicative ₹170 secondary price does not establish executable entry. **Counterevidence:** An illustrative surviving path may meet some hurdles. That result cannot fill an actual evidence gap.\\n\\n**D:** Material permanent-impairment risk exists under credible linked customer, collection and margin stress. Customer-group and connected-counterparty concentrations interact with thin available cash. The bear and slower-collection models quantify unfunded gaps and bounded recapitalization. Book equity is not a liquid recovery floor: stock, plant, advances and aged receivables can lose value together. **Counterevidence:** Moderate drawn leverage and recent primary capital provide a buffer. Documented bank headroom could improve resilience; certain failure is not claimed.\\n\\n**P:** NS: Reverse expectations depend on unsupported actual entry cash, capacity ramp and terminal economics. Revenue-growth scans retain every failed candidate path. Terminal EV is earned from FCFF and capital requirements, not automatic P/E rerating. Reported P/E is a cross-check, not a substitute for intrinsic value. **Counterevidence:** Mid-teens historical revenue growth and recent investment can create value if cash conversion improves.\\n\\n**L:** NS: Primary BSE20/60-session traded values and current restrictions are missing. S5 supports the1000-share reference lot and current shares, not a BSE trading series. Secondary19October ₹170/2000-share observations cannot replace60 verified sessions. The ₹5 lakh reference cannot be represented exactly by whole lots: two cost ₹3.4 lakh and three ₹5.1 lakh. **Counterevidence:** The stock has reported trading. Missing NSE issuer rows do not prove suspension or zero BSE liquidity.\\n\\nThe factor evidence distinguishes different questions: GOV assesses conduct and disclosure; CASH assesses realization; EARN assesses operating and per-share profit; CAP remains unknown rather than punishing an unreconciled denominator. D3 describes material impairment under linked stress, not a claim of actual insolvency. R and P cannot be recovered from an attractive-looking hypothetical rescued return. No composite is ranking eligible.\\n\\n**Judgment sensitivity:** GOV and MOAT each ±1, with all other factors fixed. GOV3 would activate the maximum-3 governance cap only if adverse evidence justified that judgment. The exercises are not competing factual scores.\\n\\n| Changed factor | Change | BQS cappedbound | GQS cappedbound | IAS cappedbound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | -1 | 3.0000 to 3.0000 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 3.0000 |\\n| GOV | 1 | 4.0400 to 5.8400 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n| MOAT | -1 | 3.6400 to 5.4400 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n| MOAT | 1 | 4.0400 to 5.8400 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\nRachit supplies knitted, printed and warp-knitted mattress fabrics, pillow fabric, binding tape and fire-retardant fabric; it also performs job work and has small traded-goods revenue. Its single Meerut facility occupies approximately 3,000 square metres. Customers specify texture, design and material requirements. Revenue is recognized on delivered goods/services; commercial credit described in the RHP is commonly 60–75 days. The company must buy yarn/materials, process and stock fabric, then collect from larger downstream mattress brands. This makes working-capital quality as important as the order itself. [S3 business chapter]\\n\\nFY26 manufactured goods were ₹52.2163 crore, or about 98% of operating revenue. Trading was ₹0.5568 crore and job work ₹0.3482 crore. The RHP's FY25 product table totals ₹40.9082 crore, versus ₹41.7032 crore operating revenue, leaving ₹0.7950 crore not fully reconciled within that product presentation. This is a presentation gap, not permission to invent a segment. FY26 MD&A also uses broad blanket/mat/carpet wording inconsistent with the more specific RHP description; no new segment is assumed without a reconciliation. [S1 pp120; S3 pp146–166]\\n\\nThe geographic revenue split and a current export contribution were not adequately established from the retrieved primary records. Imported machinery and FX procurement exposure do not imply export diversification. Current market share is ND, not an analyst-created percentage of a generic textile TAM.\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\nThe strongest plausible advantage is repeat supply into customer-specific mattress applications, supported by long relationships, customization capability and a lower FY25 sales-return rate of 0.28% versus 0.77% in FY24 and 0.50% in FY23. Seven of the top ten customers reportedly had relationships exceeding ten years. This provides evidence of acceptance, but not a proprietary technology, irreplaceable qualification, consumer brand or enduring pricing power. The March 2025 MoUs are non-exclusive and do not guarantee purchases. [S3]\\n\\n**Concentration must be consolidated by economic group.** Sheela Foam represented 35.92% and Kurlon Enterprise 14.27% of FY25 sales. Independent Sheela Foam filings establish common control, so combined group exposure was **50.19%**, not two independent 36%/14% relationships. Top-five concentration rose from 67.44% in FY23 to 71.91% in FY24 and 82.55% in FY25; top-ten concentration reached 88.97%. Top supplier was 26.66%, top five 67.47%, top ten 83.61%. FY26 customer and supplier concentrations are ND. [S3,S8,S9]\\n\\n| Comparator | Relevant comparison | Important non-comparability |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Sheela Foam / Kurlon | Downstream customer bargaining power, brand demand and group concentration | Customer, not a pure-play fabric supplier; much larger branded/foam business |\\n| Arvind | Integrated fabrics, processing and utilization discipline | Denim/wovens/garments and scale differ; not mattress-fabric economics |\\n| Vardhman Textiles | Yarn/fabric integration, commodity input and capital-cycle discipline | Upstream integration and product/customer mix differ |\\n| Welspun Living | Home-textile quality, customer programs and manufacturing investment | Export/home-textile mix and scale differ materially |\\n\\nThese are **qualitative operating comparators**, supported by primary business disclosures [S8–S12], not a cherry-picked current P/E table. The RHP reports no directly comparable listed Indian peer. A numeric peer valuation ranking is withheld because matched FY26 normalization, market dates and capital structures were not independently reconstructed. Larger-company multiples cannot manufacture a Rachit moat.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\nPreferred measurement window was selected before scoring: comparable **FY21–FY26 five-year CAGR 17.36%**, ₹23.9094→₹53.2399 crore. Three-year FY23–FY26 cross-check is **18.10%**, ₹32.3221→₹53.2399 crore. Both fall in the 15–20% REV7 band. No ±1 trajectory upgrade is applied. A ten-year comparable series is ND; post-FY26 TTM is ND, with no invented quarterly cash flows or overlapping interim periods. FY21 was a pandemic-affected starting year, which is disclosed rather than silently changed to whichever window looks best.\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | Addition | EBITDA | PAT | CFO | Cash capex | FCF | Debt | Reported cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 23.91 | NS | 1.37 | 0.27 | 1.30 | 0.04 | 1.26 | 9.56 | 1.02 |\\n| 2022 | 28.75 | 4.84 | 1.22 | 0.42 | 1.18 | 2.44 | -1.26 | 11.64 | 1.06 |\\n| 2023 | 32.32 | 3.57 | 2.01 | 0.32 | -1.37 | 2.10 | -3.47 | 14.79 | 0.20 |\\n| 2024 | 37.08 | 4.76 | 4.27 | 2.03 | 6.95 | 1.15 | 5.79 | 6.38 | 0.04 |\\n| 2025 | 41.70 | 4.62 | 7.23 | 4.56 | 0.46 | 5.03 | -4.57 | 9.14 | 0.42 |\\n| 2026 | 53.24 | 11.54 | 8.50 | 4.58 | -3.60 | 9.10 | -12.70 | 10.90 | 0.37 |\\n\\nThe history uses original FY21/FY22 statements, FY23 RHP restatement, the original FY24 comparator, and latest FY25/FY26 comparative statements. CFO bases differ and are bridged below; revenue and P&L are substantially more comparable. No acquisitions or consolidation-perimeter changes were identified, so growth is treated as organic at constant perimeter, with Medium confidence. **Organic is not synonymous with unit-volume growth:** the issuer subsequently reported approximate nine-month sales of 41.93 lakh metres and ₹41 crore through December 2025, versus 35.11 lakh metres and ₹32 crore a year earlier. This is **19.42%** quantity growth and **28.13%** value growth; blended realization rises **7.29%**, from about ₹91.14 to ₹97.78 per metre. The residual is price **and mix**, not a pure price increase. This unaudited approximate update supports real sales-volume participation but is not full-year FY26 production or audited price/volume attribution. [S15]\\n\\n| Component | FY25 | FY26 | Addition | Share of addition |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| manufactured | 41.3032 | 52.2163 | 10.9131 | 94.59% |\\n| traded | 0.0412 | 0.5568 | 0.5156 | 4.47% |\\n| jobwork | 0.2544 | 0.3482 | 0.0938 | 0.81% |\\n| packing | 0.0004 | 0.0084 | 0.0080 | 0.07% |\\n| freight | 0.1040 | 0.1103 | 0.0063 | 0.05% |\\n\\nManufactured goods account for the great majority of the ₹11.5367 crore FY26 addition. Elite Traders related-party sales rose from ₹2.6286 to ₹4.6667 crore, contributing **17.67%** of the total increment. Excluding Elite sales from both years still gives **24.31%** growth, but this calculation is a concentration cross-check, not an assertion that related sales are fictitious or non-organic.\\n\\nThe FY23–FY25 shift was substantial: knitted revenue ₹14.7710→₹22.1548→₹27.2495 crore; printed revenue ₹14.4818→₹11.2497→₹8.0173 crore; warp knit ₹0.9247→₹0.7809→₹3.2225 crore. Higher-value mix and falling printed output both matter. FY25 invoiced knitted quantity of 2.1373 million metres exceeds disclosed production of 1.8850 million; differences may involve stocks, sourcing or definitions, but a clean reconciliation was not supplied. Do not infer spare capacity from that mismatch.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n| Product | FY23 capacity/output | FY24 capacity / output | FY25 capacity / output |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Knitted fabric | 1280400 / 1220676 | 1560000 / 1560000 | 1885000 / 1885000 |\\n| Printed fabric | 3024000 / 2017849 | 3024000 / 1550246 | 3024000 / 1149140 |\\n| Warp knit | 275000 / 134242 | 275000 / 127003 | 275000 / 192514 |\\n| Pillow | 2405000 / 754496 | 2405000 / 358329 | 2405000 / 328112 |\\n| Binding tape | 1000000 / 237217 | 2000000 / 1884573 | 2000000 / 488202 |\\n\\nThe disclosed utilization history is stronger for knitting than for older printed and pillow lines. Knitted capacity/output reached 1.885 million metres in FY25 at reported 100% utilization. Printed utilization fell to about 38%, pillow to about 14%, and binding-tape output was volatile. Growth is therefore a product-mix and investment story, not simply filling a uniformly idle factory. December 2025 installed knitting capacity is separately verified below; full-year line-level realized throughput and utilization remain ND. The nine-month aggregate sales quantity is not interchangeable with knitted production. [S3 p157; S14–S15]\\n\\nThe IPO envisaged ten double-jersey jacquard knitting machines plus two double-sided machines. The July 2025 vendor quote was USD675,000, translated to ₹5.8787 crore, with 30% down and 70% before shipment. Its validity ended 31 October 2025. The issuer's financing table allocated ₹5 crore before an assumed ₹1 crore subsidy, added ₹0.4 crore contingency and showed ₹4.4 crore IPO funding. It separately identified ₹0.8787 crore internal/bank contribution. Consequently **gross quoted procurement plus contingency is ₹6.2787 crore**, leaving **₹1.8787 crore beyond the ₹4.4 crore earmarked pool before any subsidy collection**. The table's arithmetic labels are imperfect; no subsidy cash, fresh sanction or usable undrawn loan is assumed here. Contingency already includes possible freight, duty/GST and commissioning costs; baseline modeling does not add tax twice. [S3 p97]\\n\\nAt 31 March 2026, ₹4.4 crore capex-object utilization was **zero**, despite the FY26 indicated schedule. The issuer allows carry-forward, but that flexibility is not proof of commissioning. FY26's separately reported ₹9.10 crore cash capex is real and must not be relabeled as use of the untouched IPO object. The SIDBI ₹7.5 crore loan is already in closing debt; it is not future funding to count again. [S1 CARO and notes]\\n\\nThe signed 1 December 2025 filing confirms a separate six-machine cohort was installed and commissioned: two 1.6EE machines add 800 metres/day, three 1.6E add 1,500, and one 2.4 adds 700. Total machines rise from 17 to 23 and stated daily knitted-fabric capacity from 6,300 to 9,300 metres, a 47.62% increase. This is positive execution evidence, not an assumption that all expansion stalled. The filing provides no cost, financing or IPO-object mapping; the completed six do not demonstrate completion of the 12-machine object that remained unspent in the later March report. [S14]\\n\\nA bottom-up scaling test still needs invoice-to-PPE and funding reconciliation, realized throughput, saleable yield, contribution per metre, payment/collection timing and customer commitments. These are not sufficiently established for cohort returns. The model therefore imposes working-capital and physical-capital cash requirements for every growth path, instead of granting revenue growth free capacity. It uses 10–18% sustainable ending productive-capital returns, with 13% base versus a 15% cost of capital, and tests changes. These are assumptions, not an evidenced CAP score. **GQS is NS and capped at 5**, rather than awarding runway points for an IPO capex headline.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\nAnupam and Naina Kansal are the two largest shareholders and executive leaders. Together they hold 3,335,500 shares according to the primary financial note. Secondary March 2026 shareholding totals promoter/group ownership at 3,341,187 shares, or 67.68%; the extra group holdings and current encumbrance status need primary pattern verification. The balance is not necessarily freely executable float. Rose Kansal is treated as promoter/non-executive, not independent: the FY26 front corporate-details table conflicts with committee/rotation/secretarial disclosures. Conflicting director identifiers in some financial-signature blocks are not copied into this research. [S1–S4,S13]\\n\\nFY26 remuneration to Anupam and Naina was ₹24 lakh each, versus ₹30 lakh each FY25. The AGM notice proposed ₹3.5 lakh monthly for each from July 2026, versus ₹2 lakh: combined annualized ₹84 lakh compared with FY26 ₹48 lakh. The 30 September AGM approved both revisions: the filed voting results and scrutinizer report show 3,353,300 valid votes for each and none against, unanimous among votes cast rather than among all outstanding shares. The voting table appears to repeat votes as shares held, so that denominator is not used for float or capitalization. All-in scenario margins include the higher remuneration; at unchanged FY26 sales the 15% base margin is ₹0.5114 crore below FY26 EBITDA, versus the ₹0.36 crore annualized pay increase, while the 17% bull margin requires operating improvement. Family/KMP compensation and related job-work transactions require disclosure and economic scrutiny, but family involvement alone is not misconduct. [S1 notice and pp123–124; S16]\\n\\n**Connected cash exposure:** Elite Traders receivable ₹2.45282 crore plus advance ₹2.3444087 crore equals **₹4.80 crore**, **104.70%** of FY26 PAT and **13.75%** of closing equity. Elite had ₹4.6667 crore sales and ₹0.9975 crore purchases. Mirahi Traders, owned by CFO Rachit Gupta, supplied ₹1.7544 crore, with ₹0.5104 crore payable. Commercial terms and independent price comparisons are not established by generic board-approved/arm's-length statements. AOC2's “no advance” presentation is not reconciled with the financial note. FY25 AOC2 also appears to mislabel aggregate RPT amounts as Elite goods transactions. These are specific evidence requests, not allegations of diversion. [S1 pp118,123–124; S2 AOC2/notes]\\n\\nThe company received ₹1.125 crore and repaid ₹1.96775 crore director loans during FY26, a ₹0.84275 crore net repayment. Repaying disclosed financing is not automatically leakage, but its timing alongside IPO funding and weak CFO is relevant to minority capital-allocation assessment. CFO Garima Moorjani resigned 29 November 2025 and Rachit Gupta was appointed 8 December; the secretarial report records this change. [S1]\\n\\n| Dated promise / disclosure | Observed delivery | Assessment |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23–FY25 knitted expansion | Output 1.221→1.560→1.885 million metres | Clear positive operating scaling |\\n| August 2025 RHP: ₹9.5 crore working capital | Reported fully used March 2026; actual advances/inventory rose sharply | Use does not establish productive recovery |\\n| ₹1.32 crore loan prepayment | Reported fully utilized | Gross debt nevertheless rose with new SIDBI financing; no net-deleveraging claim |\\n| Six additional knitting machines | Commissioned 1 December 2025; stated capacity rises 6,300→9,300 metres/day | Positive, verified delivery; realized utilization and cost mapping unknown |\\n| FY26 IPO machine program ₹4.4 crore | Zero utilization at March 2026; deposit retained | Missed initial timetable; require updated milestones |\\n| Continued cash generation from growth | FY25 near-zero CFO, FY26 negative CFO | Profit growth has not established cash delivery |\\n| FY25 audit-trail observation | Recurs FY26 | Remediation unproven |\\n\\nHistorical CRISIL disclosure is relevant but dated: on 25 March 2021 the ₹6.4 crore facility rating remained B/Stable **Issuer Not Cooperating** and was withdrawn at the company's request with bank NOC. It is neither a current rating nor evidence of a current default. [S7]\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n### Audit scope and reliability\\n- **FY22 original:** Rajeev Devendra & Co., 25 September 2022, unmodified opinion. Full CARO reviewed. Separate IFC reporting explicitly not applicable, not an affirmative clean IFC opinion. No reported default/fraud/cash loss; inventory verification and bank-return agreement described. [S4 pp11–18]\\n- **FY23–FY25 RHP:** full restatement report and financial annexures reviewed. FY23 tax auditors explicitly reported **no stock records** and missing MSME-payable information. Restatement is not reissuance of original statutory reports. Original FY23/FY24 full audit/IFC/CARO packets could not be retrieved. [S3 F1–F42]\\n- **FY25 and FY26 originals:** Singhal Gupta & Co. LLP issued unmodified statutory and IFC opinions. Nevertheless both years disclose that audit-trail functionality did not operate throughout all transactions, with preservation/tampering assurance unavailable. This matters even without a modified headline opinion. [S1 pp95–105; S2 audit]\\n- FY26 CARO tax disputes total **₹0.5157943 crore**, versus **₹0.4882129 crore** in the note, an unresolved ₹0.0275814 crore difference. CARO financial-institution-dues wording also conflicts with the explicit SIDBI loan note. MSME interest was not provided based on past trend/materiality. FY26 gratuity expense figures differ between note and P&L/CFO. These inconsistencies are recorded proportionately; no fraud finding is manufactured.\\n- FY26 secretarial audit, including its limiting annexure, reports general compliance but does not verify financial books, future viability or operational efficacy. Environmental/labour compliance relies partly on management representation. [S1 pp61–68]\\n\\n### Earnings-to-cash bridge\\nFY26 operating EBIT is PBT + finance cost − all other income = ₹6.7213263 crore; operating EBITDA adds ₹1.7760902 crore depreciation to reach ₹8.4974165 crore. Other income of ₹0.2779542 crore is interest, excluded from operating economics. FY25 asset-sale gain and earlier rebates are also conservatively excluded. Normalized operating tax is an analyst 25.17%, with no assertion of actual tax-law eligibility.\\n\\n| FY26 CFO component | ₹cr |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| pbt | 6.2098984 |\\n| da | 1.7760902 |\\n| finance | 0.7893821 |\\n| gratuity | 0.0783598 |\\n| remove_interest_income | -0.2779542 |\\n| inventory | -4.2977787 |\\n| receivables | -3.2668173 |\\n| advances | -4.9071962 |\\n| other_ca | 0.8589346 |\\n| payables | 1.4305152 |\\n| other_cl | 0.3413797 |\\n| cash_tax | -2.3371640 |\\n| reported_rounding_residual | 0.0000001 |\\n\\nThe bridge includes a separately identified ₹1 source arithmetic/rounding residual and reconciles to negative ₹3.6023503 crore CFO. Inventory absorbed ₹4.2978 crore, receivables ₹3.2668 crore, and advances ₹4.9072 crore. **Capex is below CFO and cannot explain this negative CFO.** Reported cash capex was ₹9.09896 crore, giving negative ₹12.70131 crore FCF before financing-classified interest. Placement of deposits is investing cash flow, not machine capex.\\n\\nFY26 cash reconciliation: opening ₹0.4203996 + CFO −3.6023503 + investing −15.4293305 + financing 18.9778322 = closing ₹0.3665510 crore. Financing comprised net equity ₹18.0050878, loan proceeds ₹8.985, repayments −₹7.2228735 and interest −₹0.7893821. Net debt therefore did not disappear after the IPO.\\n\\n### Preserve cash vintages rather than hiding them\\n| Item | Original / earlier | Later presentation | Treatment |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY22 closing cash / FY23 RHP opening | ₹1.0637072 crore | ₹1.3971 crore | Scope differs by ₹0.3333928; close to noncurrent-deposit placement. Exact reconciliation unresolved. Short ICICI FDR largely backs overdraft, not free liquidity. |\\n| FY23 cash | ₹0.1005324 crore in FY25 original comparator opening | RHP ₹0.2038 crore | RHP includes short deposit about ₹0.1032 crore; rounding retained. |\\n| FY24 CFO | ₹6.9458603 crore | RHP ₹9.1871 crore | ₹2.2412397 crore gap; approximately ₹2.2492 crore OCL/short-debt financing reclassification is the main bridge, not improved collections. Small residual not fabricated away. |\\n| FY25 CFO | ₹0.4831967 crore original | ₹0.463076 latest; ₹0.4603 RHP | ₹0.0201207 investment-interest reclassification; RHP additionally includes ₹0.0028 deposit movement and rounding. |\\n| FY25 debt | ₹9.2342631 crore original | ₹9.135746 crore latest | ₹0.0985171 issued cheques moved into OCL, not debt forgiven. DTA also netted against DTL. |\\n| FY24 PAT restatement | RHP original ₹2.0534 crore | ₹2.0299 crore | Prior-year tax is a subtraction; F15 plus sign is inconsistent. |\\n\\nThe selected three-year CFO/PAT ratio of sums is **34.07%** and five-year **30.33%**. Even substituting the more favorable RHP FY24 CFO yields only **49.14%** five-year conversion. The result remains weak under either presentation; it does not depend on choosing the pessimistic vintage. Three-year reported FCF totals ₹-11.48 crore and five-year ₹-16.22 crore. Interest-classification consistency is also visible in canonical historical rows.\\n\\n### Working capital, capital returns and recoverability\\n| FY | AR | Inventory | Advances | AP | Broad ONWC | Average AR days | Inventory days proxy | AP days proxy | CCC proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 7.37 | 5.00 | 0.77 | 4.35 | 7.84 | NS | NS | NS | NS |\\n| 2022 | 8.09 | 5.11 | 0.80 | 7.10 | 8.07 | 98.13 | 100.83 | 114.23 | 84.72 |\\n| 2023 | 6.32 | 6.57 | 1.33 | 4.72 | 11.62 | 81.39 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| 2024 | 6.51 | 3.59 | 0.10 | 6.21 | 6.05 | 63.13 | 78.58 | 84.54 | 57.18 |\\n| 2025 | 7.94 | 4.23 | 0.05 | 3.35 | 11.31 | 63.20 | 57.32 | 70.07 | 50.45 |\\n| 2026 | 11.20 | 8.53 | 4.96 | 4.78 | 21.81 | 65.61 | 69.69 | 44.43 | 90.87 |\\n\\nAverage AR days use average opening/closing receivables divided by annual operating revenue. Inventory/payable days use materials + traded purchases + stock changes as a consistently disclosed **incomplete COGS proxy**, not a false full cost-of-sales figure. CCC is consequently diagnostic. Where FY23 comparable expense detail was not standardized, days remain ND.\\n\\nAt March 2026, ₹0.9541905 crore receivables were over three years old, including ₹0.3840185 crore disputed over-three-year balances. All were still considered good. An insurance claim of ₹0.2887693 crore persists from at least FY21. Those facts do not prove zero recovery, but recovery cannot be assumed at par without collection evidence. Inventory rose from ₹4.2321 to ₹8.5299 crore, well ahead of revenue, and short advances from ₹0.0510 to ₹4.9582 crore. [S1 notes14–18; S4]\\n\\n| FY | EBIT | NOPAT | Broad IC | IC residual | Average ROIC proxy | Average ROCE proxy | ROE | Interest cover |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 0.89 | 0.67 | 10.96 | 0.08 | NS | NS | NS | 1.21 |\\n| 2022 | 0.74 | 0.55 | 13.15 | 0.02 | 4.57% | 5.91% | 14.52% | 1.66 |\\n| 2023 | 1.17 | 0.87 | 17.97 | -0.01 | 5.62% | 7.37% | 9.87% | 1.33 |\\n| 2024 | 3.32 | 2.48 | 11.78 | -0.04 | 16.68% | 22.23% | 45.64% | 5.51 |\\n| 2025 | 6.38 | 4.77 | 21.08 | -0.13 | 29.05% | 38.87% | 51.34% | 17.20 |\\n| 2026 | 6.72 | 5.03 | 38.90 | -0.15 | 16.77% | 20.23% | 19.41% | 8.51 |\\n\\nBroad operating-capital proxy = AR + inventory + advances + other current assets − AP − OCL − provisions + PPE + CWIP. Financial proxy = equity + debt − reported cash − disclosed deposits. Their residual is shown rather than silently declared zero: tax, nonoperating claims, cash definitions and deferred-tax classifications differ. Deposits include operating securities and restricted FDR, so neither proxy is a clean enterprise invested-capital measure. Average ROCE uses equity + debt − cash; ROE uses average equity. These labels are distinct.\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | ΔIC | Incremental ROIC proxy | Net investment | Bridge residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2025–2026 | 0.26 | 17.82 | 1.44% | 17.82 | 0.00 |\\n| 2023–2026 | 4.16 | 20.93 | 19.85% | 21.88 | -0.95 |\\n| 2021–2026 | 4.36 | 27.94 | 15.62% | 28.89 | -0.95 |\\n\\nThe FY25–FY26 broad incremental return is only about 1.44%; the three-year recovery-window proxy is about 19.85% and five-year about 15.62%, versus a 13–17% illustrative capital-cost range. The variation is economically meaningful: a trough recovery cannot be extrapolated as the return on the latest machine cohort. The three/five-year investment bridge retains roughly ₹0.95 crore residual, reflecting asset disposals/subsidies and reporting definitions. **CAP remains NS**, not a mechanically high score from the most flattering window.\\n\\n### Available liquidity, obligations and dilution\\nReported cash ₹0.366551 includes ₹0.1390228 crore deposited-but-uncleared cheques. Excluding those leaves **₹0.2275282 crore**, including bank cash-credit debit balance ₹0.1838588 and cash on hand ₹0.0436694. Federal FDR ₹4.6370795 crore includes ₹4.5 crore principal and accrual; at least ₹4.4 crore is committed to the IPO object. SBI FDR ₹1.9657479 crore, principal ₹1.875 crore, is pledged to SIDBI. The model excludes the pledged balance and Federal excess/accrual from freely available operating and terminal cash. It does not assume that a debit cash-credit balance means a sanctioned undrawn limit is usable. [S1]\\n\\nTerm debt is ₹10.8978726 crore; ₹2.524554 crore is current maturity. Individual rates range roughly 7.3–8.16%; repayment tables and the aggregate maturity need a precise bank schedule. Stale net debt/EBITDA excluding restricted cash is 1.26×, and operating interest coverage exceeds 8×. These are favorable accounting cross-checks, not proof that intraperiod bills can be met. Tax disputes, accrued interest, statutory dues and customer/credit concentration are separate obligations. Factory land is described as leased from UPSIDC; no material Ind AS lease capitalization is invented under AS. Lease/commitment schedules beyond available disclosure remain a gap.\\n\\n| FY | Closing shares | Published EPS | Qualification |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 191000 | 13.97 /  | Later10:1split and3:4bonus imply17.5economic-equivalent shares per original share; EPS not directly comparable. |\\n| 2022 | 191000 | 22.03 /  | Same17.5factor; no economic dilution from split/bonus. |\\n| 2023 | 191000 | .97 /  | Restated split/bonus-equivalent EPS. |\\n| 2024 | 191000 | 6.07 /  | Restated EPS on later split/bonus-equivalent basis. |\\n| 2025 | 3628100 | 13.03 /  | Published weighted-average3,499,776 versus closing3,628,100; private placement285,600at₹80 generated₹2.2848cr. |\\n| 2026 | 4937100 | 10.51 / 10.70 | Front and note disagree. NotePAT/4,282,600=10.70; 4September2025IPO timing does not obviously reproduce denominator. Current issued-share EPS separately computed; no invented correction. |\\n\\nThe 10:1 split and 3:4 bonus are not economic dilution. The FY25 private placement and FY26 IPO are. Actual IPO issue was **1,309,000**, not the proposed RHP 1,308,000 shares, at ₹149, gross ₹19.5041 crore. Net proceeds ₹18.0050878 crore imply ₹1.4990122 crore issue costs. Current shares increased **36.08%** from FY25; new IPO investors represent **26.51%** of post-issue shares. PAT grew only **0.44%**. Current-issued-share EPS is **₹9.28**, versus FY25 closing-share EPS ₹12.57, a **-26.19%** change. Neither the published 10.51 nor 10.70 EPS should be used without addressing the denominator inconsistency. No outstanding options/warrants/convertibles were identified in reviewed records; absence is qualified, not an invented zero across unreviewed filings.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n### Methods and cross-checks\\nThe explicit model is a five-year **cash-constrained investor exit-value model**, with terminal enterprise value earned from normalized FCFF, not an asserted exit multiple. Its cross-checks are current-share reported P/E **18.32×**, normalized unlevered-operating-profit capitalization **16.69×** (not a levered P/E), P/B **2.41×**, stale cash-only EV/EBITDA **11.13×**, and reported FCF yield **-15.13%**. Stale EV ₹94.60 crore combines the October quote with March debt/available cash and must not be called actual entry EV. No unverified deposit or IPO cash is added twice. Negative FCF makes a positive cash-yield valuation unsupported.\\n\\n**Do not interpret the tables as a forecast of actual default or a target price.** The March-to-October actual cash bridge is missing. The model's assumptions are explicit and its output is conditional. All ordinary base/bear/bull returns are suppressed when an unfunded cash-floor breach occurs, even if later counterfactual year-end cash becomes positive. R and IAS remain NS.\\n\\n| Case | Growth | Margin | AR/inventory/APdays | Terminal revenue | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR | New shares | Retained ownership |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2.00% | 11.00% | 107/120/40 | 49.96 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| base | 12.00% | 15.00% | 77/90/50 | 93.83 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| bull | 20.00% | 17.00% | 70/75/50 | 139.10 | NS | 7.78% | 745029 | 86.89% |\\n\\n### Cash, investment and financing mechanics\\n1. Start on 31 March 2026 with **₹0.2275282 crore available cash**, ₹10.8978726 crore drawn debt and exactly 4,937,100 shares. Itemized economic ONWC is **₹21.2776169 crore**, excluding income-tax assets, investment interest, financing/market-making prepayments and the old insurance claim; it includes recoverable GST, ordinary operating prepayments, supplier advances and operating liabilities. Excluded claims are not quietly monetized.\\n2. Three linked pre-entry periods end 30 June, 30 September and 20 October; twenty post-entry quarters follow. Every cash event carries the preceding event's closing cash. Year0 is a 203-day bridge, not a full annual forecast. The base allocates 50% operating receipts before bills, the remainder afterward. This convention is tested; it is not observed intra-quarter timing.\\n3. Pay the assumed 30% machine deposit before entry, the remaining 70% in the first post-entry quarter. Release earmarked funds only against scheduled procurement. Prepayment earns no depreciation until the assumed first post-entry commissioning. This is a dated sensitivity assumption for the outstanding 12-machine IPO object, **not evidence that those machines actually arrived**. The six commissioned in December 2025 are within the opening March balance-sheet perimeter and are not charged again as a completed-cohort purchase; exact invoice and object mapping is still unavailable.\\n4. Cash taxes are based on positive EBIT less interest at 25.17%, with no cash loss-tax refund. Settle opening statutory/interest arrears separately. Repay disclosed current maturities straight-line to March 2027; assume remaining term principal amortizes through March 2031. Actual contractual amortization and access to bank headroom are ND. An accelerated-term repayment stress is included.\\n5. ONWC follows explicit AR, inventory and payable days plus other operating capital. Every growth year funds productive-capital catch-up **before growth receipts**. Sustainable capital-return parameters refer to ending productive capital, not historical average ROIC. Legacy DA models a maintained replacement base; growth assets and maintenance spending above DA increase subsequent depreciation. Maintenance is 1.10× DA baseline, tested at 1.50×. Scheduled procurement already contains ₹0.4 crore contingency; a separate extra-18%-GST stress has no assumed refund.\\n6. **Optional rescue is not committed finance.** Only after entry, issue the minimum whole shares at fixed ₹136, a 20% discount to the indicative quote, with 3% fees, at each breach. Total gross cap ₹20 crore and total authorized shares 8,000,000 both constrain issuance. Authorized headroom is not shareholder/regulatory approval. Once a breach cannot be funded, no subsequent recap is allowed to erase it; values/IRRs stay null. Existing shareholders do not subscribe in this model, so ownership dilution is explicit.\\n7. Terminal productive-capital catch-up, depreciation, growth reinvestment, maintenance above DA and additional cash taxes/GST are modeled. FCFF uses the larger of NOPAT×g/terminalROIC and physical capital-intensity growth, plus maintenance above DA. Terminal growth 2/3/4% is below WACC 17/15/13%. A four-quarter continuation ledger determines a cash reserve before excess cash can be added to equity. Unused/restricted deposits remain excluded. No dividend is counted and retained again.\\n\\n| Case | First floor breach | Largest gap | Rescue gross / fees | Rescue failed | Terminal price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2026-06-30 | 15.95 | 0.00 / 0.00 | True | NS |\\n| base | 2026-06-30 | 6.35 | 0.00 / 0.00 | True | NS |\\n| bull | 2027-01-20 | 9.83 | 10.13 / 0.30 | False | 247.22 |\\n\\nThe following annual rows retain every ordinary and hypothetical rescued path. Rows after a failure are **counterfactual diagnostics**, not a repaired solvent forecast. Negative cash is an unmet financing need, never a debt plug.\\n\\n**Bear, ordinary**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 25.17 | 2.77 | 0.99 | 0.33 | 4.36 | 3.44 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | -4.77 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 46.16 | 5.08 | 2.58 | 0.46 | 0.51 | 8.33 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -11.12 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 47.08 | 5.18 | 2.61 | 0.52 | 0.52 | 2.87 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -12.46 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 48.02 | 5.28 | 2.63 | 0.58 | 0.53 | 2.90 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -13.62 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 48.98 | 5.39 | 2.66 | 0.64 | 0.54 | 2.92 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | -14.61 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 49.96 | 5.50 | 2.69 | 0.70 | 0.56 | 2.95 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -15.52 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Base, ordinary**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 29.61 | 4.44 | 0.99 | 0.75 | -0.01 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 0.86 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 59.63 | 8.94 | 2.42 | 1.47 | 2.55 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -1.73 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 66.78 | 10.02 | 2.45 | 1.78 | 2.86 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -1.65 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 74.80 | 11.22 | 2.47 | 2.11 | 3.20 | 2.72 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -0.90 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 83.77 | 12.57 | 2.51 | 2.49 | 3.58 | 2.94 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 0.38 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 93.83 | 14.07 | 2.81 | 2.83 | 4.01 | 5.80 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -0.50 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Bull, ordinary**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 31.09 | 5.29 | 0.99 | 0.96 | -2.48 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 3.96 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 67.08 | 11.40 | 2.42 | 2.09 | 3.76 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | 2.00 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 80.50 | 13.68 | 2.45 | 2.70 | 4.51 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | 3.17 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 96.60 | 16.42 | 2.58 | 3.40 | 5.41 | 3.91 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | 4.44 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 115.92 | 19.71 | 3.09 | 4.14 | 6.50 | 8.19 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 3.04 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 139.10 | 23.65 | 3.70 | 5.02 | 7.80 | 9.82 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | 1.01 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Bear, conditional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 25.17 | 2.77 | 0.99 | 0.33 | 4.36 | 3.44 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | -4.77 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 46.16 | 5.08 | 2.58 | 0.46 | 0.51 | 8.33 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -11.12 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 47.08 | 5.18 | 2.61 | 0.52 | 0.52 | 2.87 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -12.46 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 48.02 | 5.28 | 2.63 | 0.58 | 0.53 | 2.90 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -13.62 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 48.98 | 5.39 | 2.66 | 0.64 | 0.54 | 2.92 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | -14.61 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 49.96 | 5.50 | 2.69 | 0.70 | 0.56 | 2.95 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -15.52 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Base, conditional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 29.61 | 4.44 | 0.99 | 0.75 | -0.01 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 0.86 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 59.63 | 8.94 | 2.42 | 1.47 | 2.55 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -1.73 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 66.78 | 10.02 | 2.45 | 1.78 | 2.86 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -1.65 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 74.80 | 11.22 | 2.47 | 2.11 | 3.20 | 2.72 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -0.90 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 83.77 | 12.57 | 2.51 | 2.49 | 3.58 | 2.94 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 0.38 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 93.83 | 14.07 | 2.81 | 2.83 | 4.01 | 5.80 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -0.50 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Bull, conditional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 31.09 | 5.29 | 0.99 | 0.96 | -2.48 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 3.96 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 67.08 | 11.40 | 2.42 | 2.09 | 3.76 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | 4.10 | 5096515 |\\n| 2 | 80.50 | 13.68 | 2.45 | 2.70 | 4.51 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | 5.75 | 5132503 |\\n| 3 | 96.60 | 16.42 | 2.58 | 3.40 | 5.41 | 3.91 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | 7.55 | 5173135 |\\n| 4 | 115.92 | 19.71 | 3.09 | 4.14 | 6.50 | 8.19 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 9.48 | 5424974 |\\n| 5 | 139.10 | 23.65 | 3.70 | 5.02 | 7.80 | 9.82 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | 10.84 | 5682129 |\\n\\n| Case | Productive capital | Required capital | Catch-up capex | Growth reinvestment | Maintenance above DA | Cash reserve | Terminal FCFF | Earned EV / EBIT |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 54.74 | 20.90 | 0.00 | 1.09 | 0.28 | 1.24 | 0.76 | 1.82 |\\n| base | 65.17 | 64.73 | 0.00 | 2.00 | 0.29 | 1.35 | 6.38 | 4.72 |\\n| bull | 83.37 | 82.85 | 0.00 | 3.45 | 0.39 | 2.09 | 11.68 | 6.51 |\\n\\nAll investor value comes from the terminal period because no distributions are assumed. Earned terminal EV/EBIT is a result of the capital/cash assumptions, not an independent valuation input. The 17% WACC/2% growth case normalizes capitalization downward rather than assuming a rerating.\\n\\n### Sensitivities and reverse expectations\\n| Change | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR | Rescue shares | Unfunded rescue |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| All March cash, including uncleared cheques | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Uncleared cheques excluded; available cash haircut50% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Tax claims settled | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Only25% early collections | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| No early collections | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Deposit unavailable | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Term debt accelerated | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| AR30 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Inventory30 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Suppliers cut15 days of credit | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| EBITDA3 points below base | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| EBITDA3 points above base | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Growth capex25% overrun | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Maintenance1.5 times DA | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Interest11% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| New-asset DA18% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Extra18% cash GST above quote contingency | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Opening CWIP never productive | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Aged AR25% and stock10% cash loss | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Elite advance50% cash loss | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| WACC13% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| WACC17% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Terminal growth2% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Terminal growth4% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Capital returns10% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Capital returns18% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Execution1% entry and5% exit | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Bull post-entry cash loss ₹3cr | NS | 6.93% | 972439 | False |\\n| Bull post-entry cash loss ₹10cr | NS | NS | 1470588 | True |\\n| Bull post-entry cash loss ₹30cr | NS | NS | 1470588 | True |\\n| All pre-entry operating receipts first, optimistic timing ceiling | NS | -8.56% | 481025 | False |\\n\\n| Pre-entry timing | Growth | Margin | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 50% early | 2.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 2.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 2.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 12.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 12.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 12.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 20.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 20.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 20.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 100% early | 2.00% | 12.00% | NS | -28.30% |\\n| 100% early | 2.00% | 15.00% | NS | -17.02% |\\n| 100% early | 2.00% | 18.00% | NS | -9.57% |\\n| 100% early | 12.00% | 12.00% | NS | -15.42% |\\n| 100% early | 12.00% | 15.00% | NS | -8.56% |\\n| 100% early | 12.00% | 18.00% | NS | -7.14% |\\n| 100% early | 20.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 100% early | 20.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 100% early | 20.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n\\nThe additional **100% early pre-entry receipt** bridge is a deliberately optimistic cash-timing ceiling: it assumes all modeled operating cash arrives before pre-entry bills, changes no actual disclosed bank balance, and retains 50% early post-entry receipts. It is not management guidance and does not cure G3. It allows the reverse exercise to distinguish “price demands strong economics” from “the ordinary cash bridge already fails.” Baseline 50%-early failed candidates are retained in full.\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | Hurdle | Conditional entry price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | True | 12.00% | 140.28 |\\n| bull | True | 15.00% | 122.91 |\\n| bull | True | 18.00% | 108.06 |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | True | 12.00% | 61.66 |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | True | 15.00% | 54.03 |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | True | 18.00% | 47.50 |\\n\\nThese entry prices use a fixed ₹136 rescue issue price. They are not dynamically self-consistent forecasts of a different future financing price, and not buy thresholds. A discount cannot repair unavailable financing or unreliable opening evidence. The earlier base 1% entry/5% exit stress remains NS because its cash path fails. To show a material price impact on paths that can survive under conditional financing, the following table applies 0%, 1%, 3% and 5% **each side**. Costs represent illustrative combined spread, market impact and charges, not observed BSE spreads. They reduce investor proceeds or increase entry outlay without changing company financing. Cash-failed ordinary paths remain NS; personal taxes are excluded.\\n\\n| Case | Entry cost | Exit cost | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR | Rescue shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Bull | 0.00% | 0.00% | NS | 7.78% | 745029 |\\n| Bull | 1.00% | 1.00% | NS | 7.35% | 745029 |\\n| Bull | 3.00% | 3.00% | NS | 6.49% | 745029 |\\n| Bull | 5.00% | 5.00% | NS | 5.64% | 745029 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 0.00% | 0.00% | NS | -8.56% | 481025 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 1.00% | 1.00% | NS | -8.93% | 481025 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 3.00% | 3.00% | NS | -9.65% | 481025 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 5.00% | 5.00% | NS | -10.37% | 481025 |\\n\\n| Pre-entry timing | Target | Rescue | Growth roots | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 50% early | 0.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 0.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 15.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 15.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 0.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 0.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 15.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 15.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 5× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 5× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 10× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 10× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 5× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 5× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 10× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 10× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n\\nThe search scans growth −10% to +80%, at 2.5-point steps, then bisects only adjacent financeable brackets to a stated residual tolerance. It retains failed brackets/candidates and does not join across insolvency gaps. “No root” means no valid bracket in this bounded search, not a theorem that no off-grid solution exists. **252 unique complete paths** are retained once in the canonical registry, with every scenario, grid, root attempt and hurdle pointing to its full annual, quarterly, event and terminal ledger. Registry deduplication never drops a failed root path.\\n\\n### Implementation is a separate unknown\\nOne lot at ₹170 costs ₹1.7 lakh. A ₹5 lakh reference is 2.941 lots: two lots cost ₹3.4 lakh, three ₹5.1 lakh. The secondary 19 October observation is only 2,000 shares and ₹3.41 lakh turnover. At 5% of that one day's volume the allowance is 100 shares, below a single lot. Theoretical one-day-based liquidation estimates would be 73.31/29.33/14.66 days at 2%/5%/10%, but **these are explicitly not 60-session estimates and earn no L score**. Official20/60 means, medians, zero sessions, spread and current BSE restrictions remain ND. A missing NSE row cannot be called a BSE zero-trade day.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n**Impairment risk: High, with material uncertainty, not proven distress.** The linked adverse path is: a common-control customer group cuts orders or negotiates price → knitted utilization and margin fall → stock and receivables/advances take longer to recover → the remaining machine procurement and term repayments consume cash before collections → fresh equity is unavailable or issued below intrinsic value → the original shareholder's claim is permanently diluted, or operations must shrink at distressed asset values. Book equity is not a liquid recovery floor because the same stress can impair stock, plant usefulness and customer/RPT claims together.\\n\\nThe explicit bear combines 15% lower entry run-rate, 2% subsequent growth, 11% EBITDA margin, AR107 days, inventory120 days, suppliers40 days, 25% procurement overrun, ₹1.2 crore cash loss, 17% WACC and 10% capital return. It breaches the modeled floor before entry, so **no ordinary bear terminal price or percentage price downside is quoted**. A fabricated −X% target would hide the unresolved financing. The full ledger instead quantifies the gap, its timing and how much optional capped recapitalization can or cannot fund.\\n\\n**Single most important thesis-break variable:** realizable operating cash after connected-party advances, collections, inventory and mandatory payments, not headline revenue. A disclosed unapproved diversion, falsified bank balance or failed obligation would escalate the gate; none is presumed here.\\n\\n**Strongest contrary evidence:** core manufactured revenue genuinely grew, historical knit output expanded, profits are mainly operating, substantial primary equity was raised, drawn leverage is not extreme relative to EBITDA, and available auditors did not report default or fraud. An orderly, documented conversion of advances/stock to cash and functioning bank limits could make the cash story much less adverse. The research is therefore a hold pending evidence, not a confident fraud/insolvency accusation. No differentiated market insight is claimed merely because the shares are small or thinly traded.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\nFive-year price growth required for 5× is about **37.97% annualized**, and for 10× about **58.49%**. Those are returns on each share, not enterprise sales growth. More machines, higher nominal sales or a larger market capitalization can coexist with a lower value per old share after dilution.\\n\\nThe canonical reverse tests solve for terminal per-share multiples 5× and 10× after all modeled capital spending, debt service, continuation reserve, capped whole-share issuance and earned FCFF valuation. Targets correspond to ₹850 and ₹1,700 per current reference share, before execution costs. Ordinary failed paths are not given multibagger returns. The separate optimistic pre-entry timing scenario is explicitly conditional and its required growth, terminal revenue/capital, dilution and earned EV are available through each solution's full path.\\n\\nThe enterprise-to-equity route is preferred to blindly holding current P/E constant. It does not assume that ₹6.2787 crore procurement supports any arbitrary sales number; it funds productive-capital intensity and growth ONWC. At each accepted root, assess implied revenue against 17.36% five-year historical growth, customer access, supplier capacity and the existing 1.885 million-metre knitting base. Current market share, future machine throughput and committed orders are unavailable, so no numeric TAM share is fabricated. **Classification: Speculative / unsupported**, even if an optimistic conditional search produces a mathematical solution. There is no defensible success probability.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\nThese are research checkpoints, not recurring reminders or trading instructions.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Evidence / milestone | Warning | Thesis-break / escalation | Review source |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Actual cash and bank availability | March available cash₹0.2275cr; annual principal₹2.5246cr | Reconciled March-to-entry bank/CF bridge, sanctioned undrawn limits and restrictions | Reliance on unverified CC or earmarked FDR for ordinary bills | Failed mandatory payment or false bank balance | Next financial filing plus bank evidence |\\n| Elite recovery | AR₹2.4528cr + advance₹2.3444cr | Subsequent delivery/settlement by counterparty and aging | Exposure grows faster than external sales or cash is rolled indefinitely | Evidence of unrecoverable/unauthorized diversion | RPT note and settlement proof |\\n| Aged/disputed AR | >3y₹0.9542cr, disputed>3y₹0.3840cr | Actual collections, credit-loss policy | Further aging without collections or unchanged “good” assertions | Material loss not recognized or customer failure | Next audited aging |\\n| Inventory and supplier advances | Inventory₹8.5299cr; advances₹4.9582cr | Conversion into production/sales/cash, stock records | Both grow materially faster than revenue again | Inventory verification failure or non-delivery of major advances | Interim/annual notes, inventory audit |\\n| Remaining IPO machines | Six other machines commissioned December 2025; ₹4.4 crore IPO object unspent March 2026; quote expired October 2025 | Fresh quote, deposit/invoice dates, installation and output | Another reporting period without a dated commissioning plan | Unauthorized object diversion or unfunded unavoidable completion | Utilization filing and commissioning evidence |\\n| Knit volume and customer concentration | FY25 output 1.885m metres; December 2025 daily capacity 9,300m; nine-month aggregate sales quantity +19.42%; Sheela/Kurlon 50.19% | FY26/27 line-level output, external customers, realized pricing | Growth only in related sales or further group dependence | Permanent loss of group demand without replacement economics | Customer/product disclosures |\\n| Earnings per share | FY26 current-shareEPS₹9.28 vs₹12.57FY25 | Profit growth after new-capital denominator; EPS reconciliation | Revenue growth but recurring EPS contraction | New shares repeatedly fund losses, not productive capital | Results and capital filings |\\n| Reporting remediation | Audit trail exception two years; CFO/notes inconsistent | Auditor-signed reconciliation and functioning full-year audit trail | Repeated boilerplate contradictions | Qualified cash/inventory assurance or substantiated manipulation | Audit/CARO/IFC and secretarial annexures |\\n| Tradability | Primary20/60-sessionBSE series missing; lot1000 | Date-aligned primary tape, zero sessions, spread/restrictions | Lot-level exit cannot fit5% participation | Suspension/effectively untradeable conditions | BSE master, bhavcopy and surveillance filings |\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n**Hold — unresolved / Pending evidence. Research priority only; not eligible for a favorable new-entry classification or any of the three ranked lists.** All three composite scores are NS. The compounder conditions fail independently: GOV/MOAT below7, CASH3, CAP/BAL unscored, material critical gates unresolved. The capped GQS bound does not create a completed score.\\n\\nNo buy/sell quantity, portfolio allocation or execution recommendation is inferred because position, cost basis, tax circumstances and actual BSE liquidity are not supplied. For an existing holder, the unresolved issues are financial reliability, cash realization, financing and implementation, not merely a high P/E. A lower price changes conditional hurdle arithmetic but does not cure those gates.\\n\\nTo reopen a favorable assessment, obtain: (1) an exact current cash/debt/availability bridge, (2) full original FY23/FY24 audit packets and CFO-vintage reconciliation, (3) connected-party collection/delivery evidence and independent commercial terms, (4) verified procurement/completion of the remaining IPO object and cash-funded economics of both machine cohorts, and (5) primary BSE60-session execution data. A genuine adverse finding should change the relevant factor/gate once, not create indiscriminate deductions everywhere.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n### Source ledger\\n**S1. FY2025-26 Annual Report, Rachit Prints Limited**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/07092026/5d4ef9a5-0163-4d50-b6ea-0a73e15d3a4b.pdf). Printed pages95–98: statutory report;99–100: IFC;101–105: CARO;106–127: statements and notes;61–68: secretarial report and limiting letter;76–79: AOC2;85–87: remuneration;35–57: board report and MD&A. Relevant complete reports and annexures reviewed. Publication: 2026-09-07; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 76f9f18ddedf6fbd609e9cff8d33734650e9257766a82d8da15ab6d7f5a16d17.\\n\\n**S2. FY2024-25 original signed Annual Report, despite filename21st**. [Source](https://img1.wsimg.com/blobby/go/2cdeb469-2004-4614-81a6-b71018d5d34f/21st%20Annual%20Report.pdf). Complete statutory audit, IFC, CARO and financial notes. P&L, balance sheet, cash flow and EPS notes27–29; board report and AOC2. Both FY25 and FY24 comparatives retained. Publication: 2025-07-22; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 98e49da26b5cabdd5c14c88430313deb7082203417595ecb774111a4f0b8e06b.\\n\\n**S3. August2025 Red Herring Prospectus with FY23–FY25 restated statements**. [Source](https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/sep-2025/1756985406401_935.pdf). Pages31–51: risks;92–102: objects, quote and funding;146–166: business, customers and capacity;171–196: management and capital;201–243(F1–F42): complete restatement report and financial notes; litigation and approvals chapters. Restatement is not a reissued original audit. Publication: 2025-08-21; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 b712cf1e7a1eb12a7a3d8c3bc442259cb3e913f1ad6fe2ffb5429c235c591e96.\\n\\n**S4. FY2021-22 original signed Annual Report with FY21 comparatives**. [Source](https://img1.wsimg.com/blobby/go/2cdeb469-2004-4614-81a6-b71018d5d34f/annual%20report%202021-22.pdf). PDF pages11–14: audit;15–18: CARO;19: balance sheet;20: P&L;21: cash flow;22–31: notes. All31 pages reviewed; key statement images visually checked against OCR. Publication: 2022-09-25; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 23b352262f776dc5ba4c683fc81e307061f91e4dcca18e930bde0aa23489c4aa.\\n\\n**S5. 19October2026 official NSE interop security reference**. [Source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz). Exact ISIN rows: eligible SM and ineligible ST. Official reference master only, not BSE trades or evidence of an NSE listing. Publication: 2026-10-19; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 d3665d120c8b0379df3483321ad45f8230535c7124b9759de5c29961df326898.\\n\\n**S6. 19October2026 dated secondary price observation**. [Source](https://choiceindia.com/stocks/rachit-prints-ltd-share-price). ₹170 close,2000 shares and ₹341000 turnover, corroborated by Investing historical data. Not primary execution evidence. Publication: 2026-10-19; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S7. CRISIL historic bank-rating withdrawal, information non-cooperation**. [Source](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RachitPrintsPrivateLimited_March%2025%2C%202021_RR_267605.html). Complete rationale, history and facility annexure: ₹6.4 crore, B/Stable Issuer Not Cooperating, withdrawn at company request with bank NOC. Historical, not a current rating or default. Publication: 2021-03-25; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 9e875511faa8c315bc8f2c861db29cdc7f7c219653effb6646964eed4ef0acbb.\\n\\n**S8. Sheela Foam FY25 earnings-call transcript**. [Source](https://backend.sheelafoam.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Transcript-Conference-Call.pdf). Management discusses the first full financial year of combined Sheela Foam and Kurlon operations. Supports common customer-group risk. Publication: 2025-05-19; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 59e80450626c95165503dfd71f11bd3b7d40bb397ea356fcf1917c9187a3d461.\\n\\n**S9. Sheela Foam filing identifying parent control of Kurlon Enterprise**. [Source](https://backend.sheelafoam.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Resignation_of_Auditor_from_KEL_Intimation.pdf). December2023 ownership-change correspondence identifies Sheela Foam as the parent company. Publication: 2023-12-04; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 f5623d5505b04116571e0eff6b1475f094ec941c507432a01cec85113c5d4dc5.\\n\\n**S10. Arvind FY25 operating review**. [Source](https://www.arvind.com/sites/default/files/field_quarterly_reports_file/Media%20Release%20for%20the%20period%20ended%2031.03.2025.pdf). Fabric and garment integration comparator; not a direct mattress-fabric peer or current valuation benchmark. Publication: 2025-05-15; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S11. Vardhman Textiles FY25 Annual Report**. [Source](https://www.vardhman.com/Document/Report/Financials/Annual/Vardhman%20Textiles%20Ltd/Annual_Report_2024-25.pdf). Directors report on yarn and grey-fabric production; upstream/integrated textile comparator only. Publication: 2025; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S12. Welspun Living FY25 Annual Report**. [Source](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/72b21553-63e0-48f0-9241-468030bab177.pdf). Home-textile business and global customer base; not a direct mattress-fabric valuation peer. Publication: 2025-08-14; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S13. Secondary shareholding cross-check**. [Source](https://money.rediff.com/amp/companies/Rachit-Prints-Ltd/17080271/share-holding). 31March2026 promoter holdings of3,341,187 out of4,937,100 shares, rounded67.68%. Primary complete pattern and encumbrance verification remain outstanding. Publication: 2026-03-31; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S14. Completed six-machine knitting capacity expansion, 1 December 2025**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/01122025/d7341027-2be6-445b-a243-7db0bb8d05c3.pdf). Both pages read. Installation and commissioning of six machines completed on 1 December 2025; installed machines rise from 17 to 23 and stated daily knitted-fabric capacity from 6,300 to 9,300 metres. No cost, financing or IPO-object mapping supplied. Publication: 2025-12-01; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 e6bd14597308ee008d10bd60d8a7ad76edf14b0caebb178f28b906cb530a3756.\\n\\n**S15. Nine-month sales performance update, 2 January 2026**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/02012026/8884d5f0-fc6a-40c0-98c5-ca6cc7944c7e.pdf). Full one-page filing read. Approximate nine-month sales quantity and value for April–December 2025 and comparative 2024. Not audited full-year production, product mix, margins or cash flow. Publication: 2026-01-02; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 c57217263059cb839ed0c4e03a0aaedfaef531b02af1cfcc60b3b6cce1c85758.\\n\\n**S16. September 2026 AGM voting results and complete scrutinizer report**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/04102026/cc8dcbc1-8ddf-4838-b290-1b9d33fd18bd.pdf). All 22 pages reviewed, including scanned scrutinizer report on pages 17–22. Resolutions 5 and 6 approve remuneration revisions; 3,353,300 valid votes for, zero against. Voting table shares-held denominators are inconsistent with issued capital and are not used for float or capitalization. Page 2 expressly records NSE NOTLISTED. Publication: 2026-10-04 (letter dated 3 October; AGM 30 September); retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 2e11eff9b789bed6e1037616ebfcf4b57d00591efecbfc43cd20a9919f56a786.\\n\\n### Explicit gaps and retrieval limits\\nBSE original filing/tape endpoints returned HTTP403; the FY26 original-document mirror was identity-checked, not replaced with an aggregator's financial summary. The historical BSE DRHP endpoint and SME variant failed; lead-manager “PDF” endpoints returned HTML fallbacks. Guessed issuer asset names for FY23/FY24 originals returned404, and the current issuer domain is parked. Failure records are retained in audit_evidence. These failures establish missing evidence, not adverse business facts.\\n\\nFY21/FY22 original statements required OCR; key BS/CF images were visually checked, and unit scaling cross-checked to share face value and the board's revenue totals. Original statements use a different cash/deposit scope from the RHP. No raw source PDF, OCR dump, helper, private transport ID or credential is included in the user-facing recovery packages.\\n\\n### Assumption and calculation dictionary\\n- **Fact:** explicitly reported in an identified source; **calculation:** reproducible from cited facts; **assumption:** scenario cash timing, growth, margin, days, tax, maintenance, amortization, WACC, terminal growth/return and hypothetical equity terms. Unknown actual bank headroom, subsidies, orders and remaining IPO-object commissioning are never assumptions presented as facts. The six-machine December 2025 completion is separately identified as a sourced fact.\\n- **CFO/PAT:** ratio of cumulative sums. **Operating EBIT:** PBT + finance cost − other income. **NOPAT:** operating EBIT×(1−normalizedtax). **FCF:** reported CFO−cashcapex, with financing-classified interest separately visible. **ROIC/ROCE:** average denominators except explicitly labeled ending productive-capital assumptions. **Incremental return:** ΔNOPAT/ΔIC, with nonpositive denominator NM, no favorable score from an unreliable proxy.\\n- **Cash breach:** modeled cash below analyst₹0.10cr floor at any event. It suppresses ordinary terminal equity/price/IRR, not merely the year-end balance. The breach date is the modeled period/event endpoint, not a claimed observed default date.\\n- **Rescue:** minimum whole shares rounded up for net cash need, constrained by both authorized-share headroom and gross-funding ceiling, with fees deducted. No pre-entry rescue, no post-failure resurrection, no unspecified debt plug, no optional finance called committed.\\n- **Terminal:** next NOPAT minus growth investment, maintenance above DA and unrefunded incremental taxes/GST; enterprise value FCFF/(WACC−g); equity deducts surviving debt and adds only excess unencumbered cash after liquidity reserve. Model excludes nonoperating balances without established realizability.\\n- **Precision:** original rounded RHP lakhs are not promoted into false exact source precision. Decimal scenario outputs are arithmetic precision, not forecast accuracy. Displayed figures may differ by rounding; canonical.json is authoritative.\\n\\n### Reproducibility and update log\\nThe source-safe authored inputs, engine and a separately authored arithmetic verifier regenerate the complete report, canonical object, model paths, all-path cash-event CSV and validation. The packaged verifier is distinct from the external reviewer’s private reconstruction and does not claim to contain that review. Compact and full packages are restore products, not additional evidence that management's claims are true. Every candidate path is keyed once; no root scan or failed attempt loses its complete economic ledger. Validation removes prior attestations and never relies on them to establish success.\\n\\n| Research date | Change | Effect |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 23 October 2026, cutoff 20 October | Original FY22/FY25/FY26 and RHP read; exact issuer/IPO reconciled | Common v3.0 research record created, not a continuation of a previous numeric rating |\\n| 23 October 2026, same cutoff | Full 1 December 2025 commissioning filing added during independent review | EXEC revised from 4 to 5; BQS bounds improve to 3.84–5.64; all scores remain NS and classification unchanged. Added material each-side investor execution-cost sensitivity. |\\n| 23 October 2026, same cutoff | Nine-month volume update and complete AGM voting/scrutinizer filing added | Growth wording narrowed to acknowledge reported quantity growth; remuneration revisions confirmed approved. REV7, GOV4 and all gates unchanged. |\\n| 23October2026 | Cash/cheque/debt vintages and common-control customer group reconciled as far as evidence permits | CASH3, G2/G3 holds; no invented clean CAP/BAL |\\n| 23October2026 | Intraperiod funding, whole-share rescue, productive-capital/continuation and complete reverse registry implemented | Unsupported ordinary returns suppressed; hypothetical financing clearly conditional |\\n| 23October2026 | Independent review feedback on prepayment DA, amortization and maintained-capital depreciation incorporated | Model mechanics corrected before final validation; no upgrade in factual confidence |\\n\\n**Decision-changing open question:** can this company convert the expanded asset and advance base into sustained cash and value per existing share without relying on another favorable funding event? The current evidence does not answer that confidently enough to rank or recommend it.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"**Hold — unresolved. A capacity-expansion and cash-conversion thesis, not an evidence-backed compounder.** Rachit makes specialty mattress fabrics and has shifted toward higher-value knitted fabric. Operations grew at 17.36% over FY21–FY26, but FY26's ₹53.24 crore sales produced only ₹4.58 crore PAT and negative ₹3.60 crore operating cash flow. The decisive concern is not the small listing: it is the combination of customer-group concentration, substantial related-party advances/receivables, unspent IPO expansion money, thin available cash, and recurring reporting-control weaknesses. At the indicative 19 October 2026 price of ₹170, reported P/E is 18.32×, which does not establish a margin of safety. BQS, GQS and IAS remain **NS**, with disclosed coverage and bounds. Current bank liquidity, independently reconciled cash vintages, recoverability of connected-party balances and verified completion/economics of the still-unspent IPO object are prerequisites for a favorable investment conclusion. A cheaper quote alone cannot resolve these gaps.\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"- **Exact security:** Rachit Prints Limited; ISIN **INE0ZN101029**; BSE SME code **544503**, symbol **RACHIT**. This is not Rachit Finance or another similarly named issuer. Incorporated 31 March 2003; predecessor business dates to 1985; public-company conversion in July 2024. Listed 8 September 2025. [S1–S4]\\n- **Information cutoff:** 20 October 2026. **Market date:** 19 October 2026. Research assembled 23 October from documents already published before cutoff. Later price/shareholding observations are not used to revise the snapshot.\\n- **Indicative price:** ₹170, corroborated by two secondary dated observations. **Current shares:** 4,937,100. **Market capitalization:** ₹83.93 crore. Lot: 1,000 shares, corroborated by the official interop reference; this is **not evidence of an NSE listing or a verified BSE trading tape**. [S1,S5,S6]\\n- Standalone Indian GAAP / Accounting Standards, not Ind AS. No subsidiary, associate or joint venture identified in the reviewed issuer records. Manufacturing/textiles rubric. All financial tables are ₹ crore unless stated; financial statements denominated in hundreds were divided by 100,000.\\n- Five-year investor horizon to 20 October 2031; nominal INR equity hurdle 15%, tested at 12% and 18%. No interim distributions assumed. ₹5 lakh liquidity reference is methodological, not a recommended position for this user.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Recurring control/disclosure weakness and connected-party cash exposure. No substantiated diversion or serious minority abuse established. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Historical cash vintages, FY23 stock records, recurring audit-trail limitation and financial-note inconsistencies require clarification. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Actual March-to-entry cash and bank availability are unknown. A simulated floor breach is not an actual default; optional rescue is not committed. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | No primary BSE20/60-session series or current restrictions. The NSE reference is not a BSE tape. |\\n\\n**Confidence:** High on issuer, actual IPO share count and the transcription of FY25/FY26 signed statements; Medium on underlying revenue history, customer concentration and reported profits; Low on clean incremental returns, actual entry-date liquidity, current BSE execution conditions, and supported intrinsic return. Unmodified opinions do not erase specific audit-trail observations. Missing original FY23/FY24 full audit reports are not substituted with a management summary or an IPO restatement opinion.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"Framework: **Small / Micro-Cap Stock Research Master Prompt v3.0**, verified SHA256 `68d4de4255427df276318d0231530b867a453e740c74150e9768bf1387224137`. Nine business/growth factors plus R/D/P/L are scored once and reused. Decimal arithmetic uses 44-digit precision; display rounding is half-up, never classification input. No missing factor is replaced with a midpoint or redistributed weight.\\n\\n| Score | Known sum | Coverage | Raw / final | Uncapped bound | Capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.6400 | 80.00% | NS / NS | 3.8400 to 5.6400 | 3.8400 to 5.6400 |\\n| GQS | 4.8500 | 85.00% | NS / NS | 5.0000 to 6.3500 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 |\\n| IAS | 0.6000 | 20.00% | NS / NS | 1.4000 to 8.6000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n\\nBQS weights are GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS weights are REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS weights are final BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%.\\n\\nFor each score, known contribution K and missing weight m produce the missing-evidence interval **[K+m, K+10m]**, not a confidence interval. BQS has K=3.64, m=0.20, so 3.84–5.64. GQS has K=4.85, m=0.15, so uncapped 5.00–6.35. **Persistent CASH3 caps GQS at 5; the collapsed 5.00–5.00 bound does not turn an incomplete score into a scored 5.0.** This is supported by weak multi-year cash realization under both cash vintages, not merely FY26 expansion spending. IAS has only D scored, so generic bounds are 1.40–8.60. Propagating the known BQS bounds narrows the dependency-aware IAS range to **2.11–7.51**, still NS. No active governance/funding failure cap is asserted: G1 is Conditional and actual funding failure is unproven.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | BQS term | GQS term | IAS term |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | — | — |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | — | — |\\n| REV | 7 | Medium | 0.8400 | 2.8000 | — |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | 0.5000 | 1.0000 | — |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | NS | NS | — |\\n| CASH | 3 | Medium | 0.3000 | 0.3000 | — |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | NS | — | — |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | 0.4000 | — | — |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | — | 0.7500 | — |\\n| R | NS | Low | — | — | NS |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | — | — | 0.6000 |\\n| P | NS | Low | — | — | NS |\\n| L | NS | Low | — | — | NS |\\n\\n**GOV:** Recurring disclosure and control weakness, and concentrated connected-party cash, without demonstrated diversion. S1: The audit-trail exception recurs despite unmodified statutory and IFC opinions. S1: Elite receivables and advances total ₹4.7972 crore; purchases from CFO-owned Mirahi total ₹1.7544 crore. Commercial terms have not been independently verified. S1/S2: AOC2, EPS and tax notes contain inconsistencies. S7 records historical information non-cooperation. S16 confirms shareholder approval of the notice remuneration revisions. The voting denominator presentation is inconsistent and is not used to alter issued shares. **Counterevidence:** Auditors did not report fraud or default. The RPTs are disclosed, and no material diversion has been substantiated. These facts do not justify an automatic GOV3 or G1 failure.\\n\\n**MOAT:** Customization and repeat customers are useful but contestable, with negotiating power concentrated downstream. S3: Seven of the top ten customers had relationships exceeding ten years. S3/S8/S9: The Sheela Foam/Kurlon group represented 50.19% of FY25 revenue, while the top five customers represented 82.55%. S3: Non-exclusive MoUs contain no guaranteed orders. Standard imported machinery does not establish a proprietary cost or technology advantage. **Counterevidence:** Knitted-product growth and the FY25 sales-return rate of 0.28% demonstrate customer acceptance. The business is not wholly undifferentiated.\\n\\n**REV:** The preferred five-year comparable revenue CAGR, FY21–FY26, falls in the 15–20% band. No trajectory adjustment. S4/S3/S2/S1: Operating revenue rose from ₹23.9094 crore to ₹53.2399 crore at an unchanged standalone perimeter, with no acquisition identified. The FY23–FY26 three-year cross-check falls in the same 15–20% band. Manufacturing dominates the FY26 addition. S15 reports approximate nine-month quantity growth of 19.42% and value growth of 28.13%; product-mix and full-year price/volume attribution remain unavailable. TAM earns no score bonus. **Counterevidence:** The pandemic-affected FY21 base and FY23 stock-record limitation reduce confidence. Connected-party sales contribute to the latest increment.\\n\\n**DUR:** There is real access to mattress-fabric demand, limited by customer concentration and unverified economics of further capacity. S3: Knitted-fabric capacity grew and reported FY25 utilization was 100%. S14: Six machines were commissioned in December 2025, increasing stated daily knitted-fabric capacity from 6,300 to 9,300 metres. S1/S3: The separate 12-machine IPO object was unspent at March 2026; its quoted price expired in October 2025 and completion has not been verified. **Counterevidence:** The completed six-machine cohort, FY26 PPE investment and manufactured-sales growth demonstrate expansion activity. No blanket assertion that every growth program stalled is justified.\\n\\n**CAP:** NS: Additional-capital returns cannot be separated reliably into installed cohorts, working-capital reclassifications and trough recovery. Three- and five-year NOPAT/capital bridges are provided as diagnostics, not clean unit returns. FY26 book capital grew sharply while operating EBIT improved only modestly. FY23 stock records, FY24 cash/debt vintages and non-operating balances impair precise capital attribution. **Counterevidence:** Older assets and customers generated a strong FY24–FY25 profit recovery. This does not prove that all new investment destroys value.\\n\\n**CASH:** Persistent weak realization, not just one expansion year: five-year conversion is weak under both original and favorable restated CFO presentations. FY23 CFO was negative; FY25 CFO was near zero despite ₹4.56 crore PAT; FY26 CFO was negative ₹3.60 crore. FY24 positive CFO included stock/advance releases and classification changes, rather than a demonstrated steady cash engine. S1: Aged and disputed receivables remain classified as good, alongside substantial supplier and related-party advances. **Counterevidence:** FY26 investment could produce later collections, and FY24 did include genuine working-capital release. Nevertheless capex does not explain negative CFO, and the multi-year pattern supports the GQS cap at5.\\n\\n**BAL:** NS: Actual entry cash and confirmed bank availability are missing. The March balance sheet alone cannot establish survival. S1: Available cash before further haircut is ₹0.2275 crore, against annual term principal of ₹2.5246 crore. S1: The ₹4.4 crore IPO deposit is earmarked; the ₹1.9657 crore SBI deposit is pledged; drawn debt is ₹10.8979 crore. No automatic access to an undrawn facility, released collateral, subsidy or promoter rescue is assumed. **Counterevidence:** Post-IPO equity of ₹34.8962 crore, moderate accounting leverage and no reported default argue against claiming proven insolvency.\\n\\n**EXEC:** Mixed delivery: demonstrated product scaling and a completed six-machine cohort, against the delayed IPO object and weak cash delivery. S3/S14: Knitted output grew from 1.221 to 1.885 million metres in FY23–FY25; six further machines were commissioned on 1 December 2025. S1/S3: The RHP indicated FY26 procurement for the 12-machine IPO object, versus zero capex-object utilization at March 2026. The completed six cannot be mapped to that object without invoices and funding records. FY26 manufacturing growth did not deliver current-share earnings or cash progress. S15 corroborates nine-month sales quantity growth, but does not establish realized output or utilization by machine cohort. **Counterevidence:** The completed six machines increased stated daily capacity by 3,000 metres. Together with ₹9.10 crore FY26 cash capex, this supports EXEC5 rather than the earlier incomplete-evidence score of 4. Realized line-level utilization and cohort returns remain unknown.\\n\\n**EARN:** Profits are mainly operating, but latest per-share progress is mixed and normalization matters. FY26 other income of ₹0.278 crore is small relative to ₹6.210 crore PBT; gains are not the dominant source. FY26 revenue rose27.7% but PAT only0.44%, as depreciation and financing costs increased. The IPO increased the current share denominator36.08%. Current-share EPS is ₹9.28 versus FY25 closing-share EPS of ₹12.57. **Counterevidence:** The FY23–FY25 operating-profit recovery and current interest coverage support real earnings. Weak cash alone is not evidence of fabricated profit.\\n\\n**R:** NS: A supported base-case investor return cannot be established without actual entry liquidity and financing evidence. The five-year cash models are explicitly conditional. Every unfunded breach suppresses ordinary IRR; optional recapitalization is never called committed. The indicative ₹170 secondary price does not establish executable entry. **Counterevidence:** An illustrative surviving path may meet some hurdles. That result cannot fill an actual evidence gap.\\n\\n**D:** Material permanent-impairment risk exists under credible linked customer, collection and margin stress. Customer-group and connected-counterparty concentrations interact with thin available cash. The bear and slower-collection models quantify unfunded gaps and bounded recapitalization. Book equity is not a liquid recovery floor: stock, plant, advances and aged receivables can lose value together. **Counterevidence:** Moderate drawn leverage and recent primary capital provide a buffer. Documented bank headroom could improve resilience; certain failure is not claimed.\\n\\n**P:** NS: Reverse expectations depend on unsupported actual entry cash, capacity ramp and terminal economics. Revenue-growth scans retain every failed candidate path. Terminal EV is earned from FCFF and capital requirements, not automatic P/E rerating. Reported P/E is a cross-check, not a substitute for intrinsic value. **Counterevidence:** Mid-teens historical revenue growth and recent investment can create value if cash conversion improves.\\n\\n**L:** NS: Primary BSE20/60-session traded values and current restrictions are missing. S5 supports the1000-share reference lot and current shares, not a BSE trading series. Secondary19October ₹170/2000-share observations cannot replace60 verified sessions. The ₹5 lakh reference cannot be represented exactly by whole lots: two cost ₹3.4 lakh and three ₹5.1 lakh. **Counterevidence:** The stock has reported trading. Missing NSE issuer rows do not prove suspension or zero BSE liquidity.\\n\\nThe factor evidence distinguishes different questions: GOV assesses conduct and disclosure; CASH assesses realization; EARN assesses operating and per-share profit; CAP remains unknown rather than punishing an unreconciled denominator. D3 describes material impairment under linked stress, not a claim of actual insolvency. R and P cannot be recovered from an attractive-looking hypothetical rescued return. No composite is ranking eligible.\\n\\n**Judgment sensitivity:** GOV and MOAT each ±1, with all other factors fixed. GOV3 would activate the maximum-3 governance cap only if adverse evidence justified that judgment. The exercises are not competing factual scores.\\n\\n| Changed factor | Change | BQS cappedbound | GQS cappedbound | IAS cappedbound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | -1 | 3.0000 to 3.0000 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 3.0000 |\\n| GOV | 1 | 4.0400 to 5.8400 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n| MOAT | -1 | 3.6400 to 5.4400 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n| MOAT | 1 | 4.0400 to 5.8400 | 5.0000 to 5.0000 | 1.4000 to 8.6000 |\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"Rachit supplies knitted, printed and warp-knitted mattress fabrics, pillow fabric, binding tape and fire-retardant fabric; it also performs job work and has small traded-goods revenue. Its single Meerut facility occupies approximately 3,000 square metres. Customers specify texture, design and material requirements. Revenue is recognized on delivered goods/services; commercial credit described in the RHP is commonly 60–75 days. The company must buy yarn/materials, process and stock fabric, then collect from larger downstream mattress brands. This makes working-capital quality as important as the order itself. [S3 business chapter]\\n\\nFY26 manufactured goods were ₹52.2163 crore, or about 98% of operating revenue. Trading was ₹0.5568 crore and job work ₹0.3482 crore. The RHP's FY25 product table totals ₹40.9082 crore, versus ₹41.7032 crore operating revenue, leaving ₹0.7950 crore not fully reconciled within that product presentation. This is a presentation gap, not permission to invent a segment. FY26 MD&A also uses broad blanket/mat/carpet wording inconsistent with the more specific RHP description; no new segment is assumed without a reconciliation. [S1 pp120; S3 pp146–166]\\n\\nThe geographic revenue split and a current export contribution were not adequately established from the retrieved primary records. Imported machinery and FX procurement exposure do not imply export diversification. Current market share is ND, not an analyst-created percentage of a generic textile TAM.\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"The strongest plausible advantage is repeat supply into customer-specific mattress applications, supported by long relationships, customization capability and a lower FY25 sales-return rate of 0.28% versus 0.77% in FY24 and 0.50% in FY23. Seven of the top ten customers reportedly had relationships exceeding ten years. This provides evidence of acceptance, but not a proprietary technology, irreplaceable qualification, consumer brand or enduring pricing power. The March 2025 MoUs are non-exclusive and do not guarantee purchases. [S3]\\n\\n**Concentration must be consolidated by economic group.** Sheela Foam represented 35.92% and Kurlon Enterprise 14.27% of FY25 sales. Independent Sheela Foam filings establish common control, so combined group exposure was **50.19%**, not two independent 36%/14% relationships. Top-five concentration rose from 67.44% in FY23 to 71.91% in FY24 and 82.55% in FY25; top-ten concentration reached 88.97%. Top supplier was 26.66%, top five 67.47%, top ten 83.61%. FY26 customer and supplier concentrations are ND. [S3,S8,S9]\\n\\n| Comparator | Relevant comparison | Important non-comparability |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Sheela Foam / Kurlon | Downstream customer bargaining power, brand demand and group concentration | Customer, not a pure-play fabric supplier; much larger branded/foam business |\\n| Arvind | Integrated fabrics, processing and utilization discipline | Denim/wovens/garments and scale differ; not mattress-fabric economics |\\n| Vardhman Textiles | Yarn/fabric integration, commodity input and capital-cycle discipline | Upstream integration and product/customer mix differ |\\n| Welspun Living | Home-textile quality, customer programs and manufacturing investment | Export/home-textile mix and scale differ materially |\\n\\nThese are **qualitative operating comparators**, supported by primary business disclosures [S8–S12], not a cherry-picked current P/E table. The RHP reports no directly comparable listed Indian peer. A numeric peer valuation ranking is withheld because matched FY26 normalization, market dates and capital structures were not independently reconstructed. Larger-company multiples cannot manufacture a Rachit moat.\\n\\n\"","6","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"Preferred measurement window was selected before scoring: comparable **FY21–FY26 five-year CAGR 17.36%**, ₹23.9094→₹53.2399 crore. Three-year FY23–FY26 cross-check is **18.10%**, ₹32.3221→₹53.2399 crore. Both fall in the 15–20% REV7 band. No ±1 trajectory upgrade is applied. A ten-year comparable series is ND; post-FY26 TTM is ND, with no invented quarterly cash flows or overlapping interim periods. FY21 was a pandemic-affected starting year, which is disclosed rather than silently changed to whichever window looks best.\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | Addition | EBITDA | PAT | CFO | Cash capex | FCF | Debt | Reported cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 23.91 | NS | 1.37 | 0.27 | 1.30 | 0.04 | 1.26 | 9.56 | 1.02 |\\n| 2022 | 28.75 | 4.84 | 1.22 | 0.42 | 1.18 | 2.44 | -1.26 | 11.64 | 1.06 |\\n| 2023 | 32.32 | 3.57 | 2.01 | 0.32 | -1.37 | 2.10 | -3.47 | 14.79 | 0.20 |\\n| 2024 | 37.08 | 4.76 | 4.27 | 2.03 | 6.95 | 1.15 | 5.79 | 6.38 | 0.04 |\\n| 2025 | 41.70 | 4.62 | 7.23 | 4.56 | 0.46 | 5.03 | -4.57 | 9.14 | 0.42 |\\n| 2026 | 53.24 | 11.54 | 8.50 | 4.58 | -3.60 | 9.10 | -12.70 | 10.90 | 0.37 |\\n\\nThe history uses original FY21/FY22 statements, FY23 RHP restatement, the original FY24 comparator, and latest FY25/FY26 comparative statements. CFO bases differ and are bridged below; revenue and P&L are substantially more comparable. No acquisitions or consolidation-perimeter changes were identified, so growth is treated as organic at constant perimeter, with Medium confidence. **Organic is not synonymous with unit-volume growth:** the issuer subsequently reported approximate nine-month sales of 41.93 lakh metres and ₹41 crore through December 2025, versus 35.11 lakh metres and ₹32 crore a year earlier. This is **19.42%** quantity growth and **28.13%** value growth; blended realization rises **7.29%**, from about ₹91.14 to ₹97.78 per metre. The residual is price **and mix**, not a pure price increase. This unaudited approximate update supports real sales-volume participation but is not full-year FY26 production or audited price/volume attribution. [S15]\\n\\n| Component | FY25 | FY26 | Addition | Share of addition |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| manufactured | 41.3032 | 52.2163 | 10.9131 | 94.59% |\\n| traded | 0.0412 | 0.5568 | 0.5156 | 4.47% |\\n| jobwork | 0.2544 | 0.3482 | 0.0938 | 0.81% |\\n| packing | 0.0004 | 0.0084 | 0.0080 | 0.07% |\\n| freight | 0.1040 | 0.1103 | 0.0063 | 0.05% |\\n\\nManufactured goods account for the great majority of the ₹11.5367 crore FY26 addition. Elite Traders related-party sales rose from ₹2.6286 to ₹4.6667 crore, contributing **17.67%** of the total increment. Excluding Elite sales from both years still gives **24.31%** growth, but this calculation is a concentration cross-check, not an assertion that related sales are fictitious or non-organic.\\n\\nThe FY23–FY25 shift was substantial: knitted revenue ₹14.7710→₹22.1548→₹27.2495 crore; printed revenue ₹14.4818→₹11.2497→₹8.0173 crore; warp knit ₹0.9247→₹0.7809→₹3.2225 crore. Higher-value mix and falling printed output both matter. FY25 invoiced knitted quantity of 2.1373 million metres exceeds disclosed production of 1.8850 million; differences may involve stocks, sourcing or definitions, but a clean reconciliation was not supplied. Do not infer spare capacity from that mismatch.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"| Product | FY23 capacity/output | FY24 capacity / output | FY25 capacity / output |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Knitted fabric | 1280400 / 1220676 | 1560000 / 1560000 | 1885000 / 1885000 |\\n| Printed fabric | 3024000 / 2017849 | 3024000 / 1550246 | 3024000 / 1149140 |\\n| Warp knit | 275000 / 134242 | 275000 / 127003 | 275000 / 192514 |\\n| Pillow | 2405000 / 754496 | 2405000 / 358329 | 2405000 / 328112 |\\n| Binding tape | 1000000 / 237217 | 2000000 / 1884573 | 2000000 / 488202 |\\n\\nThe disclosed utilization history is stronger for knitting than for older printed and pillow lines. Knitted capacity/output reached 1.885 million metres in FY25 at reported 100% utilization. Printed utilization fell to about 38%, pillow to about 14%, and binding-tape output was volatile. Growth is therefore a product-mix and investment story, not simply filling a uniformly idle factory. December 2025 installed knitting capacity is separately verified below; full-year line-level realized throughput and utilization remain ND. The nine-month aggregate sales quantity is not interchangeable with knitted production. [S3 p157; S14–S15]\\n\\nThe IPO envisaged ten double-jersey jacquard knitting machines plus two double-sided machines. The July 2025 vendor quote was USD675,000, translated to ₹5.8787 crore, with 30% down and 70% before shipment. Its validity ended 31 October 2025. The issuer's financing table allocated ₹5 crore before an assumed ₹1 crore subsidy, added ₹0.4 crore contingency and showed ₹4.4 crore IPO funding. It separately identified ₹0.8787 crore internal/bank contribution. Consequently **gross quoted procurement plus contingency is ₹6.2787 crore**, leaving **₹1.8787 crore beyond the ₹4.4 crore earmarked pool before any subsidy collection**. The table's arithmetic labels are imperfect; no subsidy cash, fresh sanction or usable undrawn loan is assumed here. Contingency already includes possible freight, duty/GST and commissioning costs; baseline modeling does not add tax twice. [S3 p97]\\n\\nAt 31 March 2026, ₹4.4 crore capex-object utilization was **zero**, despite the FY26 indicated schedule. The issuer allows carry-forward, but that flexibility is not proof of commissioning. FY26's separately reported ₹9.10 crore cash capex is real and must not be relabeled as use of the untouched IPO object. The SIDBI ₹7.5 crore loan is already in closing debt; it is not future funding to count again. [S1 CARO and notes]\\n\\nThe signed 1 December 2025 filing confirms a separate six-machine cohort was installed and commissioned: two 1.6EE machines add 800 metres/day, three 1.6E add 1,500, and one 2.4 adds 700. Total machines rise from 17 to 23 and stated daily knitted-fabric capacity from 6,300 to 9,300 metres, a 47.62% increase. This is positive execution evidence, not an assumption that all expansion stalled. The filing provides no cost, financing or IPO-object mapping; the completed six do not demonstrate completion of the 12-machine object that remained unspent in the later March report. [S14]\\n\\nA bottom-up scaling test still needs invoice-to-PPE and funding reconciliation, realized throughput, saleable yield, contribution per metre, payment/collection timing and customer commitments. These are not sufficiently established for cohort returns. The model therefore imposes working-capital and physical-capital cash requirements for every growth path, instead of granting revenue growth free capacity. It uses 10–18% sustainable ending productive-capital returns, with 13% base versus a 15% cost of capital, and tests changes. These are assumptions, not an evidenced CAP score. **GQS is NS and capped at 5**, rather than awarding runway points for an IPO capex headline.\\n\\n\"","8","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"Anupam and Naina Kansal are the two largest shareholders and executive leaders. Together they hold 3,335,500 shares according to the primary financial note. Secondary March 2026 shareholding totals promoter/group ownership at 3,341,187 shares, or 67.68%; the extra group holdings and current encumbrance status need primary pattern verification. The balance is not necessarily freely executable float. Rose Kansal is treated as promoter/non-executive, not independent: the FY26 front corporate-details table conflicts with committee/rotation/secretarial disclosures. Conflicting director identifiers in some financial-signature blocks are not copied into this research. [S1–S4,S13]\\n\\nFY26 remuneration to Anupam and Naina was ₹24 lakh each, versus ₹30 lakh each FY25. The AGM notice proposed ₹3.5 lakh monthly for each from July 2026, versus ₹2 lakh: combined annualized ₹84 lakh compared with FY26 ₹48 lakh. The 30 September AGM approved both revisions: the filed voting results and scrutinizer report show 3,353,300 valid votes for each and none against, unanimous among votes cast rather than among all outstanding shares. The voting table appears to repeat votes as shares held, so that denominator is not used for float or capitalization. All-in scenario margins include the higher remuneration; at unchanged FY26 sales the 15% base margin is ₹0.5114 crore below FY26 EBITDA, versus the ₹0.36 crore annualized pay increase, while the 17% bull margin requires operating improvement. Family/KMP compensation and related job-work transactions require disclosure and economic scrutiny, but family involvement alone is not misconduct. [S1 notice and pp123–124; S16]\\n\\n**Connected cash exposure:** Elite Traders receivable ₹2.45282 crore plus advance ₹2.3444087 crore equals **₹4.80 crore**, **104.70%** of FY26 PAT and **13.75%** of closing equity. Elite had ₹4.6667 crore sales and ₹0.9975 crore purchases. Mirahi Traders, owned by CFO Rachit Gupta, supplied ₹1.7544 crore, with ₹0.5104 crore payable. Commercial terms and independent price comparisons are not established by generic board-approved/arm's-length statements. AOC2's “no advance” presentation is not reconciled with the financial note. FY25 AOC2 also appears to mislabel aggregate RPT amounts as Elite goods transactions. These are specific evidence requests, not allegations of diversion. [S1 pp118,123–124; S2 AOC2/notes]\\n\\nThe company received ₹1.125 crore and repaid ₹1.96775 crore director loans during FY26, a ₹0.84275 crore net repayment. Repaying disclosed financing is not automatically leakage, but its timing alongside IPO funding and weak CFO is relevant to minority capital-allocation assessment. CFO Garima Moorjani resigned 29 November 2025 and Rachit Gupta was appointed 8 December; the secretarial report records this change. [S1]\\n\\n| Dated promise / disclosure | Observed delivery | Assessment |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23–FY25 knitted expansion | Output 1.221→1.560→1.885 million metres | Clear positive operating scaling |\\n| August 2025 RHP: ₹9.5 crore working capital | Reported fully used March 2026; actual advances/inventory rose sharply | Use does not establish productive recovery |\\n| ₹1.32 crore loan prepayment | Reported fully utilized | Gross debt nevertheless rose with new SIDBI financing; no net-deleveraging claim |\\n| Six additional knitting machines | Commissioned 1 December 2025; stated capacity rises 6,300→9,300 metres/day | Positive, verified delivery; realized utilization and cost mapping unknown |\\n| FY26 IPO machine program ₹4.4 crore | Zero utilization at March 2026; deposit retained | Missed initial timetable; require updated milestones |\\n| Continued cash generation from growth | FY25 near-zero CFO, FY26 negative CFO | Profit growth has not established cash delivery |\\n| FY25 audit-trail observation | Recurs FY26 | Remediation unproven |\\n\\nHistorical CRISIL disclosure is relevant but dated: on 25 March 2021 the ₹6.4 crore facility rating remained B/Stable **Issuer Not Cooperating** and was withdrawn at the company's request with bank NOC. It is neither a current rating nor evidence of a current default. [S7]\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"### Audit scope and reliability\\n- **FY22 original:** Rajeev Devendra & Co., 25 September 2022, unmodified opinion. Full CARO reviewed. Separate IFC reporting explicitly not applicable, not an affirmative clean IFC opinion. No reported default/fraud/cash loss; inventory verification and bank-return agreement described. [S4 pp11–18]\\n- **FY23–FY25 RHP:** full restatement report and financial annexures reviewed. FY23 tax auditors explicitly reported **no stock records** and missing MSME-payable information. Restatement is not reissuance of original statutory reports. Original FY23/FY24 full audit/IFC/CARO packets could not be retrieved. [S3 F1–F42]\\n- **FY25 and FY26 originals:** Singhal Gupta & Co. LLP issued unmodified statutory and IFC opinions. Nevertheless both years disclose that audit-trail functionality did not operate throughout all transactions, with preservation/tampering assurance unavailable. This matters even without a modified headline opinion. [S1 pp95–105; S2 audit]\\n- FY26 CARO tax disputes total **₹0.5157943 crore**, versus **₹0.4882129 crore** in the note, an unresolved ₹0.0275814 crore difference. CARO financial-institution-dues wording also conflicts with the explicit SIDBI loan note. MSME interest was not provided based on past trend/materiality. FY26 gratuity expense figures differ between note and P&L/CFO. These inconsistencies are recorded proportionately; no fraud finding is manufactured.\\n- FY26 secretarial audit, including its limiting annexure, reports general compliance but does not verify financial books, future viability or operational efficacy. Environmental/labour compliance relies partly on management representation. [S1 pp61–68]\\n\\n### Earnings-to-cash bridge\\nFY26 operating EBIT is PBT + finance cost − all other income = ₹6.7213263 crore; operating EBITDA adds ₹1.7760902 crore depreciation to reach ₹8.4974165 crore. Other income of ₹0.2779542 crore is interest, excluded from operating economics. FY25 asset-sale gain and earlier rebates are also conservatively excluded. Normalized operating tax is an analyst 25.17%, with no assertion of actual tax-law eligibility.\\n\\n| FY26 CFO component | ₹cr |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| pbt | 6.2098984 |\\n| da | 1.7760902 |\\n| finance | 0.7893821 |\\n| gratuity | 0.0783598 |\\n| remove_interest_income | -0.2779542 |\\n| inventory | -4.2977787 |\\n| receivables | -3.2668173 |\\n| advances | -4.9071962 |\\n| other_ca | 0.8589346 |\\n| payables | 1.4305152 |\\n| other_cl | 0.3413797 |\\n| cash_tax | -2.3371640 |\\n| reported_rounding_residual | 0.0000001 |\\n\\nThe bridge includes a separately identified ₹1 source arithmetic/rounding residual and reconciles to negative ₹3.6023503 crore CFO. Inventory absorbed ₹4.2978 crore, receivables ₹3.2668 crore, and advances ₹4.9072 crore. **Capex is below CFO and cannot explain this negative CFO.** Reported cash capex was ₹9.09896 crore, giving negative ₹12.70131 crore FCF before financing-classified interest. Placement of deposits is investing cash flow, not machine capex.\\n\\nFY26 cash reconciliation: opening ₹0.4203996 + CFO −3.6023503 + investing −15.4293305 + financing 18.9778322 = closing ₹0.3665510 crore. Financing comprised net equity ₹18.0050878, loan proceeds ₹8.985, repayments −₹7.2228735 and interest −₹0.7893821. Net debt therefore did not disappear after the IPO.\\n\\n### Preserve cash vintages rather than hiding them\\n| Item | Original / earlier | Later presentation | Treatment |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY22 closing cash / FY23 RHP opening | ₹1.0637072 crore | ₹1.3971 crore | Scope differs by ₹0.3333928; close to noncurrent-deposit placement. Exact reconciliation unresolved. Short ICICI FDR largely backs overdraft, not free liquidity. |\\n| FY23 cash | ₹0.1005324 crore in FY25 original comparator opening | RHP ₹0.2038 crore | RHP includes short deposit about ₹0.1032 crore; rounding retained. |\\n| FY24 CFO | ₹6.9458603 crore | RHP ₹9.1871 crore | ₹2.2412397 crore gap; approximately ₹2.2492 crore OCL/short-debt financing reclassification is the main bridge, not improved collections. Small residual not fabricated away. |\\n| FY25 CFO | ₹0.4831967 crore original | ₹0.463076 latest; ₹0.4603 RHP | ₹0.0201207 investment-interest reclassification; RHP additionally includes ₹0.0028 deposit movement and rounding. |\\n| FY25 debt | ₹9.2342631 crore original | ₹9.135746 crore latest | ₹0.0985171 issued cheques moved into OCL, not debt forgiven. DTA also netted against DTL. |\\n| FY24 PAT restatement | RHP original ₹2.0534 crore | ₹2.0299 crore | Prior-year tax is a subtraction; F15 plus sign is inconsistent. |\\n\\nThe selected three-year CFO/PAT ratio of sums is **34.07%** and five-year **30.33%**. Even substituting the more favorable RHP FY24 CFO yields only **49.14%** five-year conversion. The result remains weak under either presentation; it does not depend on choosing the pessimistic vintage. Three-year reported FCF totals ₹-11.48 crore and five-year ₹-16.22 crore. Interest-classification consistency is also visible in canonical historical rows.\\n\\n### Working capital, capital returns and recoverability\\n| FY | AR | Inventory | Advances | AP | Broad ONWC | Average AR days | Inventory days proxy | AP days proxy | CCC proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 7.37 | 5.00 | 0.77 | 4.35 | 7.84 | NS | NS | NS | NS |\\n| 2022 | 8.09 | 5.11 | 0.80 | 7.10 | 8.07 | 98.13 | 100.83 | 114.23 | 84.72 |\\n| 2023 | 6.32 | 6.57 | 1.33 | 4.72 | 11.62 | 81.39 | NS | NS | NS |\\n| 2024 | 6.51 | 3.59 | 0.10 | 6.21 | 6.05 | 63.13 | 78.58 | 84.54 | 57.18 |\\n| 2025 | 7.94 | 4.23 | 0.05 | 3.35 | 11.31 | 63.20 | 57.32 | 70.07 | 50.45 |\\n| 2026 | 11.20 | 8.53 | 4.96 | 4.78 | 21.81 | 65.61 | 69.69 | 44.43 | 90.87 |\\n\\nAverage AR days use average opening/closing receivables divided by annual operating revenue. Inventory/payable days use materials + traded purchases + stock changes as a consistently disclosed **incomplete COGS proxy**, not a false full cost-of-sales figure. CCC is consequently diagnostic. Where FY23 comparable expense detail was not standardized, days remain ND.\\n\\nAt March 2026, ₹0.9541905 crore receivables were over three years old, including ₹0.3840185 crore disputed over-three-year balances. All were still considered good. An insurance claim of ₹0.2887693 crore persists from at least FY21. Those facts do not prove zero recovery, but recovery cannot be assumed at par without collection evidence. Inventory rose from ₹4.2321 to ₹8.5299 crore, well ahead of revenue, and short advances from ₹0.0510 to ₹4.9582 crore. [S1 notes14–18; S4]\\n\\n| FY | EBIT | NOPAT | Broad IC | IC residual | Average ROIC proxy | Average ROCE proxy | ROE | Interest cover |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 0.89 | 0.67 | 10.96 | 0.08 | NS | NS | NS | 1.21 |\\n| 2022 | 0.74 | 0.55 | 13.15 | 0.02 | 4.57% | 5.91% | 14.52% | 1.66 |\\n| 2023 | 1.17 | 0.87 | 17.97 | -0.01 | 5.62% | 7.37% | 9.87% | 1.33 |\\n| 2024 | 3.32 | 2.48 | 11.78 | -0.04 | 16.68% | 22.23% | 45.64% | 5.51 |\\n| 2025 | 6.38 | 4.77 | 21.08 | -0.13 | 29.05% | 38.87% | 51.34% | 17.20 |\\n| 2026 | 6.72 | 5.03 | 38.90 | -0.15 | 16.77% | 20.23% | 19.41% | 8.51 |\\n\\nBroad operating-capital proxy = AR + inventory + advances + other current assets − AP − OCL − provisions + PPE + CWIP. Financial proxy = equity + debt − reported cash − disclosed deposits. Their residual is shown rather than silently declared zero: tax, nonoperating claims, cash definitions and deferred-tax classifications differ. Deposits include operating securities and restricted FDR, so neither proxy is a clean enterprise invested-capital measure. Average ROCE uses equity + debt − cash; ROE uses average equity. These labels are distinct.\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | ΔIC | Incremental ROIC proxy | Net investment | Bridge residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2025–2026 | 0.26 | 17.82 | 1.44% | 17.82 | 0.00 |\\n| 2023–2026 | 4.16 | 20.93 | 19.85% | 21.88 | -0.95 |\\n| 2021–2026 | 4.36 | 27.94 | 15.62% | 28.89 | -0.95 |\\n\\nThe FY25–FY26 broad incremental return is only about 1.44%; the three-year recovery-window proxy is about 19.85% and five-year about 15.62%, versus a 13–17% illustrative capital-cost range. The variation is economically meaningful: a trough recovery cannot be extrapolated as the return on the latest machine cohort. The three/five-year investment bridge retains roughly ₹0.95 crore residual, reflecting asset disposals/subsidies and reporting definitions. **CAP remains NS**, not a mechanically high score from the most flattering window.\\n\\n### Available liquidity, obligations and dilution\\nReported cash ₹0.366551 includes ₹0.1390228 crore deposited-but-uncleared cheques. Excluding those leaves **₹0.2275282 crore**, including bank cash-credit debit balance ₹0.1838588 and cash on hand ₹0.0436694. Federal FDR ₹4.6370795 crore includes ₹4.5 crore principal and accrual; at least ₹4.4 crore is committed to the IPO object. SBI FDR ₹1.9657479 crore, principal ₹1.875 crore, is pledged to SIDBI. The model excludes the pledged balance and Federal excess/accrual from freely available operating and terminal cash. It does not assume that a debit cash-credit balance means a sanctioned undrawn limit is usable. [S1]\\n\\nTerm debt is ₹10.8978726 crore; ₹2.524554 crore is current maturity. Individual rates range roughly 7.3–8.16%; repayment tables and the aggregate maturity need a precise bank schedule. Stale net debt/EBITDA excluding restricted cash is 1.26×, and operating interest coverage exceeds 8×. These are favorable accounting cross-checks, not proof that intraperiod bills can be met. Tax disputes, accrued interest, statutory dues and customer/credit concentration are separate obligations. Factory land is described as leased from UPSIDC; no material Ind AS lease capitalization is invented under AS. Lease/commitment schedules beyond available disclosure remain a gap.\\n\\n| FY | Closing shares | Published EPS | Qualification |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 191000 | 13.97 /  | Later10:1split and3:4bonus imply17.5economic-equivalent shares per original share; EPS not directly comparable. |\\n| 2022 | 191000 | 22.03 /  | Same17.5factor; no economic dilution from split/bonus. |\\n| 2023 | 191000 | .97 /  | Restated split/bonus-equivalent EPS. |\\n| 2024 | 191000 | 6.07 /  | Restated EPS on later split/bonus-equivalent basis. |\\n| 2025 | 3628100 | 13.03 /  | Published weighted-average3,499,776 versus closing3,628,100; private placement285,600at₹80 generated₹2.2848cr. |\\n| 2026 | 4937100 | 10.51 / 10.70 | Front and note disagree. NotePAT/4,282,600=10.70; 4September2025IPO timing does not obviously reproduce denominator. Current issued-share EPS separately computed; no invented correction. |\\n\\nThe 10:1 split and 3:4 bonus are not economic dilution. The FY25 private placement and FY26 IPO are. Actual IPO issue was **1,309,000**, not the proposed RHP 1,308,000 shares, at ₹149, gross ₹19.5041 crore. Net proceeds ₹18.0050878 crore imply ₹1.4990122 crore issue costs. Current shares increased **36.08%** from FY25; new IPO investors represent **26.51%** of post-issue shares. PAT grew only **0.44%**. Current-issued-share EPS is **₹9.28**, versus FY25 closing-share EPS ₹12.57, a **-26.19%** change. Neither the published 10.51 nor 10.70 EPS should be used without addressing the denominator inconsistency. No outstanding options/warrants/convertibles were identified in reviewed records; absence is qualified, not an invented zero across unreviewed filings.\\n\\n\"","10","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"### Methods and cross-checks\\nThe explicit model is a five-year **cash-constrained investor exit-value model**, with terminal enterprise value earned from normalized FCFF, not an asserted exit multiple. Its cross-checks are current-share reported P/E **18.32×**, normalized unlevered-operating-profit capitalization **16.69×** (not a levered P/E), P/B **2.41×**, stale cash-only EV/EBITDA **11.13×**, and reported FCF yield **-15.13%**. Stale EV ₹94.60 crore combines the October quote with March debt/available cash and must not be called actual entry EV. No unverified deposit or IPO cash is added twice. Negative FCF makes a positive cash-yield valuation unsupported.\\n\\n**Do not interpret the tables as a forecast of actual default or a target price.** The March-to-October actual cash bridge is missing. The model's assumptions are explicit and its output is conditional. All ordinary base/bear/bull returns are suppressed when an unfunded cash-floor breach occurs, even if later counterfactual year-end cash becomes positive. R and IAS remain NS.\\n\\n| Case | Growth | Margin | AR/inventory/APdays | Terminal revenue | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR | New shares | Retained ownership |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2.00% | 11.00% | 107/120/40 | 49.96 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| base | 12.00% | 15.00% | 77/90/50 | 93.83 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| bull | 20.00% | 17.00% | 70/75/50 | 139.10 | NS | 7.78% | 745029 | 86.89% |\\n\\n### Cash, investment and financing mechanics\\n1. Start on 31 March 2026 with **₹0.2275282 crore available cash**, ₹10.8978726 crore drawn debt and exactly 4,937,100 shares. Itemized economic ONWC is **₹21.2776169 crore**, excluding income-tax assets, investment interest, financing/market-making prepayments and the old insurance claim; it includes recoverable GST, ordinary operating prepayments, supplier advances and operating liabilities. Excluded claims are not quietly monetized.\\n2. Three linked pre-entry periods end 30 June, 30 September and 20 October; twenty post-entry quarters follow. Every cash event carries the preceding event's closing cash. Year0 is a 203-day bridge, not a full annual forecast. The base allocates 50% operating receipts before bills, the remainder afterward. This convention is tested; it is not observed intra-quarter timing.\\n3. Pay the assumed 30% machine deposit before entry, the remaining 70% in the first post-entry quarter. Release earmarked funds only against scheduled procurement. Prepayment earns no depreciation until the assumed first post-entry commissioning. This is a dated sensitivity assumption for the outstanding 12-machine IPO object, **not evidence that those machines actually arrived**. The six commissioned in December 2025 are within the opening March balance-sheet perimeter and are not charged again as a completed-cohort purchase; exact invoice and object mapping is still unavailable.\\n4. Cash taxes are based on positive EBIT less interest at 25.17%, with no cash loss-tax refund. Settle opening statutory/interest arrears separately. Repay disclosed current maturities straight-line to March 2027; assume remaining term principal amortizes through March 2031. Actual contractual amortization and access to bank headroom are ND. An accelerated-term repayment stress is included.\\n5. ONWC follows explicit AR, inventory and payable days plus other operating capital. Every growth year funds productive-capital catch-up **before growth receipts**. Sustainable capital-return parameters refer to ending productive capital, not historical average ROIC. Legacy DA models a maintained replacement base; growth assets and maintenance spending above DA increase subsequent depreciation. Maintenance is 1.10× DA baseline, tested at 1.50×. Scheduled procurement already contains ₹0.4 crore contingency; a separate extra-18%-GST stress has no assumed refund.\\n6. **Optional rescue is not committed finance.** Only after entry, issue the minimum whole shares at fixed ₹136, a 20% discount to the indicative quote, with 3% fees, at each breach. Total gross cap ₹20 crore and total authorized shares 8,000,000 both constrain issuance. Authorized headroom is not shareholder/regulatory approval. Once a breach cannot be funded, no subsequent recap is allowed to erase it; values/IRRs stay null. Existing shareholders do not subscribe in this model, so ownership dilution is explicit.\\n7. Terminal productive-capital catch-up, depreciation, growth reinvestment, maintenance above DA and additional cash taxes/GST are modeled. FCFF uses the larger of NOPAT×g/terminalROIC and physical capital-intensity growth, plus maintenance above DA. Terminal growth 2/3/4% is below WACC 17/15/13%. A four-quarter continuation ledger determines a cash reserve before excess cash can be added to equity. Unused/restricted deposits remain excluded. No dividend is counted and retained again.\\n\\n| Case | First floor breach | Largest gap | Rescue gross / fees | Rescue failed | Terminal price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2026-06-30 | 15.95 | 0.00 / 0.00 | True | NS |\\n| base | 2026-06-30 | 6.35 | 0.00 / 0.00 | True | NS |\\n| bull | 2027-01-20 | 9.83 | 10.13 / 0.30 | False | 247.22 |\\n\\nThe following annual rows retain every ordinary and hypothetical rescued path. Rows after a failure are **counterfactual diagnostics**, not a repaired solvent forecast. Negative cash is an unmet financing need, never a debt plug.\\n\\n**Bear, ordinary**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 25.17 | 2.77 | 0.99 | 0.33 | 4.36 | 3.44 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | -4.77 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 46.16 | 5.08 | 2.58 | 0.46 | 0.51 | 8.33 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -11.12 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 47.08 | 5.18 | 2.61 | 0.52 | 0.52 | 2.87 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -12.46 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 48.02 | 5.28 | 2.63 | 0.58 | 0.53 | 2.90 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -13.62 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 48.98 | 5.39 | 2.66 | 0.64 | 0.54 | 2.92 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | -14.61 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 49.96 | 5.50 | 2.69 | 0.70 | 0.56 | 2.95 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -15.52 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Base, ordinary**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 29.61 | 4.44 | 0.99 | 0.75 | -0.01 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 0.86 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 59.63 | 8.94 | 2.42 | 1.47 | 2.55 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -1.73 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 66.78 | 10.02 | 2.45 | 1.78 | 2.86 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -1.65 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 74.80 | 11.22 | 2.47 | 2.11 | 3.20 | 2.72 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -0.90 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 83.77 | 12.57 | 2.51 | 2.49 | 3.58 | 2.94 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 0.38 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 93.83 | 14.07 | 2.81 | 2.83 | 4.01 | 5.80 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -0.50 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Bull, ordinary**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 31.09 | 5.29 | 0.99 | 0.96 | -2.48 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 3.96 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 67.08 | 11.40 | 2.42 | 2.09 | 3.76 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | 2.00 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 80.50 | 13.68 | 2.45 | 2.70 | 4.51 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | 3.17 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 96.60 | 16.42 | 2.58 | 3.40 | 5.41 | 3.91 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | 4.44 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 115.92 | 19.71 | 3.09 | 4.14 | 6.50 | 8.19 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 3.04 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 139.10 | 23.65 | 3.70 | 5.02 | 7.80 | 9.82 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | 1.01 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Bear, conditional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 25.17 | 2.77 | 0.99 | 0.33 | 4.36 | 3.44 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | -4.77 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 46.16 | 5.08 | 2.58 | 0.46 | 0.51 | 8.33 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -11.12 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 47.08 | 5.18 | 2.61 | 0.52 | 0.52 | 2.87 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -12.46 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 48.02 | 5.28 | 2.63 | 0.58 | 0.53 | 2.90 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -13.62 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 48.98 | 5.39 | 2.66 | 0.64 | 0.54 | 2.92 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | -14.61 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 49.96 | 5.50 | 2.69 | 0.70 | 0.56 | 2.95 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -15.52 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Base, conditional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 29.61 | 4.44 | 0.99 | 0.75 | -0.01 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 0.86 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 59.63 | 8.94 | 2.42 | 1.47 | 2.55 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | -1.73 | 4937100 |\\n| 2 | 66.78 | 10.02 | 2.45 | 1.78 | 2.86 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | -1.65 | 4937100 |\\n| 3 | 74.80 | 11.22 | 2.47 | 2.11 | 3.20 | 2.72 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | -0.90 | 4937100 |\\n| 4 | 83.77 | 12.57 | 2.51 | 2.49 | 3.58 | 2.94 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 0.38 | 4937100 |\\n| 5 | 93.83 | 14.07 | 2.81 | 2.83 | 4.01 | 5.80 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | -0.50 | 4937100 |\\n\\n**Bull, conditional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | DA | Tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 31.09 | 5.29 | 0.99 | 0.96 | -2.48 | 2.97 | 0.47 | 1.40 | 9.49 | 3.96 | 4937100 |\\n| 1 | 67.08 | 11.40 | 2.42 | 2.09 | 3.76 | 7.06 | 0.69 | 2.28 | 7.21 | 4.10 | 5096515 |\\n| 2 | 80.50 | 13.68 | 2.45 | 2.70 | 4.51 | 2.69 | 0.51 | 2.10 | 5.11 | 5.75 | 5132503 |\\n| 3 | 96.60 | 16.42 | 2.58 | 3.40 | 5.41 | 3.91 | 0.35 | 2.09 | 3.02 | 7.55 | 5173135 |\\n| 4 | 115.92 | 19.71 | 3.09 | 4.14 | 6.50 | 8.19 | 0.18 | 2.09 | 0.93 | 9.48 | 5424974 |\\n| 5 | 139.10 | 23.65 | 3.70 | 5.02 | 7.80 | 9.82 | 0.03 | 0.93 | 0.00 | 10.84 | 5682129 |\\n\\n| Case | Productive capital | Required capital | Catch-up capex | Growth reinvestment | Maintenance above DA | Cash reserve | Terminal FCFF | Earned EV / EBIT |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 54.74 | 20.90 | 0.00 | 1.09 | 0.28 | 1.24 | 0.76 | 1.82 |\\n| base | 65.17 | 64.73 | 0.00 | 2.00 | 0.29 | 1.35 | 6.38 | 4.72 |\\n| bull | 83.37 | 82.85 | 0.00 | 3.45 | 0.39 | 2.09 | 11.68 | 6.51 |\\n\\nAll investor value comes from the terminal period because no distributions are assumed. Earned terminal EV/EBIT is a result of the capital/cash assumptions, not an independent valuation input. The 17% WACC/2% growth case normalizes capitalization downward rather than assuming a rerating.\\n\\n### Sensitivities and reverse expectations\\n| Change | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR | Rescue shares | Unfunded rescue |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| All March cash, including uncleared cheques | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Uncleared cheques excluded; available cash haircut50% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Tax claims settled | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Only25% early collections | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| No early collections | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Deposit unavailable | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Term debt accelerated | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| AR30 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Inventory30 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Suppliers cut15 days of credit | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| EBITDA3 points below base | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| EBITDA3 points above base | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Growth capex25% overrun | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Maintenance1.5 times DA | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Interest11% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| New-asset DA18% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Extra18% cash GST above quote contingency | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Opening CWIP never productive | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Aged AR25% and stock10% cash loss | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Elite advance50% cash loss | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| WACC13% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| WACC17% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Terminal growth2% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Terminal growth4% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Capital returns10% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Capital returns18% | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Execution1% entry and5% exit | NS | NS | 0 | True |\\n| Bull post-entry cash loss ₹3cr | NS | 6.93% | 972439 | False |\\n| Bull post-entry cash loss ₹10cr | NS | NS | 1470588 | True |\\n| Bull post-entry cash loss ₹30cr | NS | NS | 1470588 | True |\\n| All pre-entry operating receipts first, optimistic timing ceiling | NS | -8.56% | 481025 | False |\\n\\n| Pre-entry timing | Growth | Margin | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 50% early | 2.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 2.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 2.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 12.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 12.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 12.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 20.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 20.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 50% early | 20.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 100% early | 2.00% | 12.00% | NS | -28.30% |\\n| 100% early | 2.00% | 15.00% | NS | -17.02% |\\n| 100% early | 2.00% | 18.00% | NS | -9.57% |\\n| 100% early | 12.00% | 12.00% | NS | -15.42% |\\n| 100% early | 12.00% | 15.00% | NS | -8.56% |\\n| 100% early | 12.00% | 18.00% | NS | -7.14% |\\n| 100% early | 20.00% | 12.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 100% early | 20.00% | 15.00% | NS | NS |\\n| 100% early | 20.00% | 18.00% | NS | NS |\\n\\nThe additional **100% early pre-entry receipt** bridge is a deliberately optimistic cash-timing ceiling: it assumes all modeled operating cash arrives before pre-entry bills, changes no actual disclosed bank balance, and retains 50% early post-entry receipts. It is not management guidance and does not cure G3. It allows the reverse exercise to distinguish “price demands strong economics” from “the ordinary cash bridge already fails.” Baseline 50%-early failed candidates are retained in full.\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | Hurdle | Conditional entry price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | True | 12.00% | 140.28 |\\n| bull | True | 15.00% | 122.91 |\\n| bull | True | 18.00% | 108.06 |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | True | 12.00% | 61.66 |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | True | 15.00% | 54.03 |\\n| base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | True | 18.00% | 47.50 |\\n\\nThese entry prices use a fixed ₹136 rescue issue price. They are not dynamically self-consistent forecasts of a different future financing price, and not buy thresholds. A discount cannot repair unavailable financing or unreliable opening evidence. The earlier base 1% entry/5% exit stress remains NS because its cash path fails. To show a material price impact on paths that can survive under conditional financing, the following table applies 0%, 1%, 3% and 5% **each side**. Costs represent illustrative combined spread, market impact and charges, not observed BSE spreads. They reduce investor proceeds or increase entry outlay without changing company financing. Cash-failed ordinary paths remain NS; personal taxes are excluded.\\n\\n| Case | Entry cost | Exit cost | Ordinary IRR | Conditional rescue IRR | Rescue shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Bull | 0.00% | 0.00% | NS | 7.78% | 745029 |\\n| Bull | 1.00% | 1.00% | NS | 7.35% | 745029 |\\n| Bull | 3.00% | 3.00% | NS | 6.49% | 745029 |\\n| Bull | 5.00% | 5.00% | NS | 5.64% | 745029 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 0.00% | 0.00% | NS | -8.56% | 481025 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 1.00% | 1.00% | NS | -8.93% | 481025 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 3.00% | 3.00% | NS | -9.65% | 481025 |\\n| Base with optimistic pre-entry timing only | 5.00% | 5.00% | NS | -10.37% | 481025 |\\n\\n| Pre-entry timing | Target | Rescue | Growth roots | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 50% early | 0.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 0.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 15.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 15.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 0.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 0.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 15.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 15.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 5× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 5× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 10× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 50% early | 10× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 5× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 5× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 10× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n| 100% early | 10× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not proof of no off-grid solution. |\\n\\nThe search scans growth −10% to +80%, at 2.5-point steps, then bisects only adjacent financeable brackets to a stated residual tolerance. It retains failed brackets/candidates and does not join across insolvency gaps. “No root” means no valid bracket in this bounded search, not a theorem that no off-grid solution exists. **252 unique complete paths** are retained once in the canonical registry, with every scenario, grid, root attempt and hurdle pointing to its full annual, quarterly, event and terminal ledger. Registry deduplication never drops a failed root path.\\n\\n### Implementation is a separate unknown\\nOne lot at ₹170 costs ₹1.7 lakh. A ₹5 lakh reference is 2.941 lots: two lots cost ₹3.4 lakh, three ₹5.1 lakh. The secondary 19 October observation is only 2,000 shares and ₹3.41 lakh turnover. At 5% of that one day's volume the allowance is 100 shares, below a single lot. Theoretical one-day-based liquidation estimates would be 73.31/29.33/14.66 days at 2%/5%/10%, but **these are explicitly not 60-session estimates and earn no L score**. Official20/60 means, medians, zero sessions, spread and current BSE restrictions remain ND. A missing NSE row cannot be called a BSE zero-trade day.\\n\\n\"","11","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"**Impairment risk: High, with material uncertainty, not proven distress.** The linked adverse path is: a common-control customer group cuts orders or negotiates price → knitted utilization and margin fall → stock and receivables/advances take longer to recover → the remaining machine procurement and term repayments consume cash before collections → fresh equity is unavailable or issued below intrinsic value → the original shareholder's claim is permanently diluted, or operations must shrink at distressed asset values. Book equity is not a liquid recovery floor because the same stress can impair stock, plant usefulness and customer/RPT claims together.\\n\\nThe explicit bear combines 15% lower entry run-rate, 2% subsequent growth, 11% EBITDA margin, AR107 days, inventory120 days, suppliers40 days, 25% procurement overrun, ₹1.2 crore cash loss, 17% WACC and 10% capital return. It breaches the modeled floor before entry, so **no ordinary bear terminal price or percentage price downside is quoted**. A fabricated −X% target would hide the unresolved financing. The full ledger instead quantifies the gap, its timing and how much optional capped recapitalization can or cannot fund.\\n\\n**Single most important thesis-break variable:** realizable operating cash after connected-party advances, collections, inventory and mandatory payments, not headline revenue. A disclosed unapproved diversion, falsified bank balance or failed obligation would escalate the gate; none is presumed here.\\n\\n**Strongest contrary evidence:** core manufactured revenue genuinely grew, historical knit output expanded, profits are mainly operating, substantial primary equity was raised, drawn leverage is not extreme relative to EBITDA, and available auditors did not report default or fraud. An orderly, documented conversion of advances/stock to cash and functioning bank limits could make the cash story much less adverse. The research is therefore a hold pending evidence, not a confident fraud/insolvency accusation. No differentiated market insight is claimed merely because the shares are small or thinly traded.\\n\\n\"","12","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"Five-year price growth required for 5× is about **37.97% annualized**, and for 10× about **58.49%**. Those are returns on each share, not enterprise sales growth. More machines, higher nominal sales or a larger market capitalization can coexist with a lower value per old share after dilution.\\n\\nThe canonical reverse tests solve for terminal per-share multiples 5× and 10× after all modeled capital spending, debt service, continuation reserve, capped whole-share issuance and earned FCFF valuation. Targets correspond to ₹850 and ₹1,700 per current reference share, before execution costs. Ordinary failed paths are not given multibagger returns. The separate optimistic pre-entry timing scenario is explicitly conditional and its required growth, terminal revenue/capital, dilution and earned EV are available through each solution's full path.\\n\\nThe enterprise-to-equity route is preferred to blindly holding current P/E constant. It does not assume that ₹6.2787 crore procurement supports any arbitrary sales number; it funds productive-capital intensity and growth ONWC. At each accepted root, assess implied revenue against 17.36% five-year historical growth, customer access, supplier capacity and the existing 1.885 million-metre knitting base. Current market share, future machine throughput and committed orders are unavailable, so no numeric TAM share is fabricated. **Classification: Speculative / unsupported**, even if an optimistic conditional search produces a mathematical solution. There is no defensible success probability.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"These are research checkpoints, not recurring reminders or trading instructions.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Evidence / milestone | Warning | Thesis-break / escalation | Review source |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Actual cash and bank availability | March available cash₹0.2275cr; annual principal₹2.5246cr | Reconciled March-to-entry bank/CF bridge, sanctioned undrawn limits and restrictions | Reliance on unverified CC or earmarked FDR for ordinary bills | Failed mandatory payment or false bank balance | Next financial filing plus bank evidence |\\n| Elite recovery | AR₹2.4528cr + advance₹2.3444cr | Subsequent delivery/settlement by counterparty and aging | Exposure grows faster than external sales or cash is rolled indefinitely | Evidence of unrecoverable/unauthorized diversion | RPT note and settlement proof |\\n| Aged/disputed AR | >3y₹0.9542cr, disputed>3y₹0.3840cr | Actual collections, credit-loss policy | Further aging without collections or unchanged “good” assertions | Material loss not recognized or customer failure | Next audited aging |\\n| Inventory and supplier advances | Inventory₹8.5299cr; advances₹4.9582cr | Conversion into production/sales/cash, stock records | Both grow materially faster than revenue again | Inventory verification failure or non-delivery of major advances | Interim/annual notes, inventory audit |\\n| Remaining IPO machines | Six other machines commissioned December 2025; ₹4.4 crore IPO object unspent March 2026; quote expired October 2025 | Fresh quote, deposit/invoice dates, installation and output | Another reporting period without a dated commissioning plan | Unauthorized object diversion or unfunded unavoidable completion | Utilization filing and commissioning evidence |\\n| Knit volume and customer concentration | FY25 output 1.885m metres; December 2025 daily capacity 9,300m; nine-month aggregate sales quantity +19.42%; Sheela/Kurlon 50.19% | FY26/27 line-level output, external customers, realized pricing | Growth only in related sales or further group dependence | Permanent loss of group demand without replacement economics | Customer/product disclosures |\\n| Earnings per share | FY26 current-shareEPS₹9.28 vs₹12.57FY25 | Profit growth after new-capital denominator; EPS reconciliation | Revenue growth but recurring EPS contraction | New shares repeatedly fund losses, not productive capital | Results and capital filings |\\n| Reporting remediation | Audit trail exception two years; CFO/notes inconsistent | Auditor-signed reconciliation and functioning full-year audit trail | Repeated boilerplate contradictions | Qualified cash/inventory assurance or substantiated manipulation | Audit/CARO/IFC and secretarial annexures |\\n| Tradability | Primary20/60-sessionBSE series missing; lot1000 | Date-aligned primary tape, zero sessions, spread/restrictions | Lot-level exit cannot fit5% participation | Suspension/effectively untradeable conditions | BSE master, bhavcopy and surveillance filings |\\n\\n\"","14","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"**Hold — unresolved / Pending evidence. Research priority only; not eligible for a favorable new-entry classification or any of the three ranked lists.** All three composite scores are NS. The compounder conditions fail independently: GOV/MOAT below7, CASH3, CAP/BAL unscored, material critical gates unresolved. The capped GQS bound does not create a completed score.\\n\\nNo buy/sell quantity, portfolio allocation or execution recommendation is inferred because position, cost basis, tax circumstances and actual BSE liquidity are not supplied. For an existing holder, the unresolved issues are financial reliability, cash realization, financing and implementation, not merely a high P/E. A lower price changes conditional hurdle arithmetic but does not cure those gates.\\n\\nTo reopen a favorable assessment, obtain: (1) an exact current cash/debt/availability bridge, (2) full original FY23/FY24 audit packets and CFO-vintage reconciliation, (3) connected-party collection/delivery evidence and independent commercial terms, (4) verified procurement/completion of the remaining IPO object and cash-funded economics of both machine cohorts, and (5) primary BSE60-session execution data. A genuine adverse finding should change the relevant factor/gate once, not create indiscriminate deductions everywhere.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"### Source ledger\\n**S1. FY2025-26 Annual Report, Rachit Prints Limited**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/07092026/5d4ef9a5-0163-4d50-b6ea-0a73e15d3a4b.pdf). Printed pages95–98: statutory report;99–100: IFC;101–105: CARO;106–127: statements and notes;61–68: secretarial report and limiting letter;76–79: AOC2;85–87: remuneration;35–57: board report and MD&A. Relevant complete reports and annexures reviewed. Publication: 2026-09-07; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 76f9f18ddedf6fbd609e9cff8d33734650e9257766a82d8da15ab6d7f5a16d17.\\n\\n**S2. FY2024-25 original signed Annual Report, despite filename21st**. [Source](https://img1.wsimg.com/blobby/go/2cdeb469-2004-4614-81a6-b71018d5d34f/21st%20Annual%20Report.pdf). Complete statutory audit, IFC, CARO and financial notes. P&L, balance sheet, cash flow and EPS notes27–29; board report and AOC2. Both FY25 and FY24 comparatives retained. Publication: 2025-07-22; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 98e49da26b5cabdd5c14c88430313deb7082203417595ecb774111a4f0b8e06b.\\n\\n**S3. August2025 Red Herring Prospectus with FY23–FY25 restated statements**. [Source](https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/sep-2025/1756985406401_935.pdf). Pages31–51: risks;92–102: objects, quote and funding;146–166: business, customers and capacity;171–196: management and capital;201–243(F1–F42): complete restatement report and financial notes; litigation and approvals chapters. Restatement is not a reissued original audit. Publication: 2025-08-21; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 b712cf1e7a1eb12a7a3d8c3bc442259cb3e913f1ad6fe2ffb5429c235c591e96.\\n\\n**S4. FY2021-22 original signed Annual Report with FY21 comparatives**. [Source](https://img1.wsimg.com/blobby/go/2cdeb469-2004-4614-81a6-b71018d5d34f/annual%20report%202021-22.pdf). PDF pages11–14: audit;15–18: CARO;19: balance sheet;20: P&L;21: cash flow;22–31: notes. All31 pages reviewed; key statement images visually checked against OCR. Publication: 2022-09-25; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 23b352262f776dc5ba4c683fc81e307061f91e4dcca18e930bde0aa23489c4aa.\\n\\n**S5. 19October2026 official NSE interop security reference**. [Source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz). Exact ISIN rows: eligible SM and ineligible ST. Official reference master only, not BSE trades or evidence of an NSE listing. Publication: 2026-10-19; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 d3665d120c8b0379df3483321ad45f8230535c7124b9759de5c29961df326898.\\n\\n**S6. 19October2026 dated secondary price observation**. [Source](https://choiceindia.com/stocks/rachit-prints-ltd-share-price). ₹170 close,2000 shares and ₹341000 turnover, corroborated by Investing historical data. Not primary execution evidence. Publication: 2026-10-19; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S7. CRISIL historic bank-rating withdrawal, information non-cooperation**. [Source](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RachitPrintsPrivateLimited_March%2025%2C%202021_RR_267605.html). Complete rationale, history and facility annexure: ₹6.4 crore, B/Stable Issuer Not Cooperating, withdrawn at company request with bank NOC. Historical, not a current rating or default. Publication: 2021-03-25; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 9e875511faa8c315bc8f2c861db29cdc7f7c219653effb6646964eed4ef0acbb.\\n\\n**S8. Sheela Foam FY25 earnings-call transcript**. [Source](https://backend.sheelafoam.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Transcript-Conference-Call.pdf). Management discusses the first full financial year of combined Sheela Foam and Kurlon operations. Supports common customer-group risk. Publication: 2025-05-19; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 59e80450626c95165503dfd71f11bd3b7d40bb397ea356fcf1917c9187a3d461.\\n\\n**S9. Sheela Foam filing identifying parent control of Kurlon Enterprise**. [Source](https://backend.sheelafoam.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Resignation_of_Auditor_from_KEL_Intimation.pdf). December2023 ownership-change correspondence identifies Sheela Foam as the parent company. Publication: 2023-12-04; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 f5623d5505b04116571e0eff6b1475f094ec941c507432a01cec85113c5d4dc5.\\n\\n**S10. Arvind FY25 operating review**. [Source](https://www.arvind.com/sites/default/files/field_quarterly_reports_file/Media%20Release%20for%20the%20period%20ended%2031.03.2025.pdf). Fabric and garment integration comparator; not a direct mattress-fabric peer or current valuation benchmark. Publication: 2025-05-15; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S11. Vardhman Textiles FY25 Annual Report**. [Source](https://www.vardhman.com/Document/Report/Financials/Annual/Vardhman%20Textiles%20Ltd/Annual_Report_2024-25.pdf). Directors report on yarn and grey-fabric production; upstream/integrated textile comparator only. Publication: 2025; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S12. Welspun Living FY25 Annual Report**. [Source](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/72b21553-63e0-48f0-9241-468030bab177.pdf). Home-textile business and global customer base; not a direct mattress-fabric valuation peer. Publication: 2025-08-14; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S13. Secondary shareholding cross-check**. [Source](https://money.rediff.com/amp/companies/Rachit-Prints-Ltd/17080271/share-holding). 31March2026 promoter holdings of3,341,187 out of4,937,100 shares, rounded67.68%. Primary complete pattern and encumbrance verification remain outstanding. Publication: 2026-03-31; retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**S14. Completed six-machine knitting capacity expansion, 1 December 2025**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/01122025/d7341027-2be6-445b-a243-7db0bb8d05c3.pdf). Both pages read. Installation and commissioning of six machines completed on 1 December 2025; installed machines rise from 17 to 23 and stated daily knitted-fabric capacity from 6,300 to 9,300 metres. No cost, financing or IPO-object mapping supplied. Publication: 2025-12-01; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 e6bd14597308ee008d10bd60d8a7ad76edf14b0caebb178f28b906cb530a3756.\\n\\n**S15. Nine-month sales performance update, 2 January 2026**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/02012026/8884d5f0-fc6a-40c0-98c5-ca6cc7944c7e.pdf). Full one-page filing read. Approximate nine-month sales quantity and value for April–December 2025 and comparative 2024. Not audited full-year production, product mix, margins or cash flow. Publication: 2026-01-02; retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 c57217263059cb839ed0c4e03a0aaedfaef531b02af1cfcc60b3b6cce1c85758.\\n\\n**S16. September 2026 AGM voting results and complete scrutinizer report**. [Source](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/04102026/cc8dcbc1-8ddf-4838-b290-1b9d33fd18bd.pdf). All 22 pages reviewed, including scanned scrutinizer report on pages 17–22. Resolutions 5 and 6 approve remuneration revisions; 3,353,300 valid votes for, zero against. Voting table shares-held denominators are inconsistent with issued capital and are not used for float or capitalization. Page 2 expressly records NSE NOTLISTED. Publication: 2026-10-04 (letter dated 3 October; AGM 30 September); retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA256 2e11eff9b789bed6e1037616ebfcf4b57d00591efecbfc43cd20a9919f56a786.\\n\\n### Explicit gaps and retrieval limits\\nBSE original filing/tape endpoints returned HTTP403; the FY26 original-document mirror was identity-checked, not replaced with an aggregator's financial summary. The historical BSE DRHP endpoint and SME variant failed; lead-manager “PDF” endpoints returned HTML fallbacks. Guessed issuer asset names for FY23/FY24 originals returned404, and the current issuer domain is parked. Failure records are retained in audit_evidence. These failures establish missing evidence, not adverse business facts.\\n\\nFY21/FY22 original statements required OCR; key BS/CF images were visually checked, and unit scaling cross-checked to share face value and the board's revenue totals. Original statements use a different cash/deposit scope from the RHP. No raw source PDF, OCR dump, helper, private transport ID or credential is included in the user-facing recovery packages.\\n\\n### Assumption and calculation dictionary\\n- **Fact:** explicitly reported in an identified source; **calculation:** reproducible from cited facts; **assumption:** scenario cash timing, growth, margin, days, tax, maintenance, amortization, WACC, terminal growth/return and hypothetical equity terms. Unknown actual bank headroom, subsidies, orders and remaining IPO-object commissioning are never assumptions presented as facts. The six-machine December 2025 completion is separately identified as a sourced fact.\\n- **CFO/PAT:** ratio of cumulative sums. **Operating EBIT:** PBT + finance cost − other income. **NOPAT:** operating EBIT×(1−normalizedtax). **FCF:** reported CFO−cashcapex, with financing-classified interest separately visible. **ROIC/ROCE:** average denominators except explicitly labeled ending productive-capital assumptions. **Incremental return:** ΔNOPAT/ΔIC, with nonpositive denominator NM, no favorable score from an unreliable proxy.\\n- **Cash breach:** modeled cash below analyst₹0.10cr floor at any event. It suppresses ordinary terminal equity/price/IRR, not merely the year-end balance. The breach date is the modeled period/event endpoint, not a claimed observed default date.\\n- **Rescue:** minimum whole shares rounded up for net cash need, constrained by both authorized-share headroom and gross-funding ceiling, with fees deducted. No pre-entry rescue, no post-failure resurrection, no unspecified debt plug, no optional finance called committed.\\n- **Terminal:** next NOPAT minus growth investment, maintenance above DA and unrefunded incremental taxes/GST; enterprise value FCFF/(WACC−g); equity deducts surviving debt and adds only excess unencumbered cash after liquidity reserve. Model excludes nonoperating balances without established realizability.\\n- **Precision:** original rounded RHP lakhs are not promoted into false exact source precision. Decimal scenario outputs are arithmetic precision, not forecast accuracy. Displayed figures may differ by rounding; canonical.json is authoritative.\\n\\n### Reproducibility and update log\\nThe source-safe authored inputs, engine and a separately authored arithmetic verifier regenerate the complete report, canonical object, model paths, all-path cash-event CSV and validation. The packaged verifier is distinct from the external reviewer’s private reconstruction and does not claim to contain that review. Compact and full packages are restore products, not additional evidence that management's claims are true. Every candidate path is keyed once; no root scan or failed attempt loses its complete economic ledger. Validation removes prior attestations and never relies on them to establish success.\\n\\n| Research date | Change | Effect |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 23 October 2026, cutoff 20 October | Original FY22/FY25/FY26 and RHP read; exact issuer/IPO reconciled | Common v3.0 research record created, not a continuation of a previous numeric rating |\\n| 23 October 2026, same cutoff | Full 1 December 2025 commissioning filing added during independent review | EXEC revised from 4 to 5; BQS bounds improve to 3.84–5.64; all scores remain NS and classification unchanged. Added material each-side investor execution-cost sensitivity. |\\n| 23 October 2026, same cutoff | Nine-month volume update and complete AGM voting/scrutinizer filing added | Growth wording narrowed to acknowledge reported quantity growth; remuneration revisions confirmed approved. REV7, GOV4 and all gates unchanged. |\\n| 23October2026 | Cash/cheque/debt vintages and common-control customer group reconciled as far as evidence permits | CASH3, G2/G3 holds; no invented clean CAP/BAL |\\n| 23October2026 | Intraperiod funding, whole-share rescue, productive-capital/continuation and complete reverse registry implemented | Unsupported ordinary returns suppressed; hypothetical financing clearly conditional |\\n| 23October2026 | Independent review feedback on prepayment DA, amortization and maintained-capital depreciation incorporated | Model mechanics corrected before final validation; no upgrade in factual confidence |\\n\\n**Decision-changing open question:** can this company convert the expanded asset and advance base into sustained cash and value per existing share without relying on another favorable funding event? The current evidence does not answer that confidently enough to rank or recommend it.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","24","48","1105","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1154","3713","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","3751","15453","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","15477","16972","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","17003","19182","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","19211","22530","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","22573","26258","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","26299","30425","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","30451","41865","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","41904","61487","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","61526","63630","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","63658","65202","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","65225","68202","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","68251","69556","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","69601","82034","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"scope\"","\"model_inputs.json\"","\"00124f0184bf4da5bbe1e98bb9be437ebb7284386cc37d376e1f33ccfb3bf9d1\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"3a87d14620a70c033f4e780299fb88731afea731c2c87db5ba3